Four Kemerovo group members were detained in Estonia and Spain
According to Postimees, police in Spain detained Slava Gulevich, the head of the so-called Kemerovo group operating in the Estonian underworld, who is known as Slava Kemerovsky in the criminal world.
Gulevich, who was detained in Spain, was arrested with the permission of the court.
Yesterday, three suspects detained in Estonia were also taken into custody.
According to suspicion, they are members of the so-called Kemerovo criminal organization between the ages of 27 and 57, who mainly commit drug crimes.
An alleged leader of a criminal organisation was detained in Mijas, Malaga Province, Spain.
Three men were detained as suspects in Estonia, one of them, according to the collected data, is the leading figure of the criminal organisation, and two are members of a criminal organisation.
The data collected indicate that tasks and roles had been clearly distributed in the criminal organisation.
The aim of the activities of the criminal organisation was to commit drug crimes and thereby earn criminal proceeds," commented Vahur Verte, head of pre-trial proceedings in the criminal case.
"We do not rule out an increase in the circle of suspects in the criminal case in question," the prosecutor added.
These are people who have been in the interest of law enforcement authorities in the past, and some of them have been sentenced for drug offences.
Verte emphasized that cooperation with Spanish colleagues has played an important role throughout the investigation.
"International cooperation has become customary for us, and this week's operation will show the success of the cooperation," prosecutor Verte commented.
Ago Leis, head of the Central Criminal Police Department, said the detentions were preceded by nearly a year and a half of preliminary investigations.
"In the first half of the week, long-term work materialised and at the same time in Estonia and Spain we detained suspicions of crimes," he said.
Leis added that officials of the Central Criminal Police had been assisted in detention in Spain.
For many years, we have had good contact and mutual understanding with the Spanish police and the Civil Guard.
These detentions are yet another message to criminals that we will catch them from the other end of Europe," Leis said.
The information gathered so far unambiguously indicates that men who have been suspected of beinging at liberty can continue to commit new crimes, which is why the prosecutor's office requested their arrest and the court also approved it.
A man arrested in Spain is awaiting his surrender to Estonia.
Real estate, vehicles and cash have been seized to ensure confiscation in this criminal case.
The pre-trial proceedings of the criminal case are conducted by the Organised Crime Bureau of the Central Criminal Police and led by the Public Prosecutor's Office.
The Tallinn City Court convicted Slava Gulevich of blackmailing in 2005 and sentenced him to five years' imprisonment.
In the middle of the last decade, Gulevich was considered the second most important man in Estonia's criminal world after the then underworld leader Nikolai Tarankov.
Gulevitš from Kemerovos rose to prominence in the Estonian underworld in the early 1990's, when he started extortions in Tallinn.
Allegedly, he taxed taxi drivers and prostitutes working at various hotels and demanded a monthly roof tax from businessmen.
International joint operations, confiscations of proceeds of crime and court judgments that have entered into force confirm that the fight against organised crime is effective.
For example, the latest verdicts, eight defendants separated from the criminal case of the so-called Dikaev criminal organisation, from whom 80 000 euros were confiscated, or a court judgment against nine persons that Igor Aleynikov created a criminal organisation in 2006 aimed at the illegal trafficking of contraband cigarettes and the commission of crimes related to human trafficking in Ida-Viru County and Southern Estonia.
The amount of confiscated criminal proceeds there is nearly 71 500 euros.
Success in these cases has been ensured by the fact that police officers exchange information with foreign colleagues every day and prosecutors exchange evidence and, if necessary, to carry out together in any country in the European Union.
Civil rights group warns against traveling to Missouri
The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) has warned black people travelling to Missouri against state discriminatory policies and racist attacks.
According to the group's statement: "NAACP travel remarks issued on Missour State, valid until August 28, 2017, invite all African-American travelers, visitors and Missourians to be especially attentive and extremely careful when moving around the state, given a series of suspicious race-based events that have taken place across the state recently and are outlined here."
NAACP said the group was prompted to issue a travel warnery by both the recent Missouri law, which makes it more difficult to win discriminating lawsuits, as well as the fact that unequitely ethnic minorities are the targets of state law enforcement.
"People's civil rights are violated.
They're being pulled to the side of the road because of their skin color, they're beatened or killed," Rod Chapel, president of NAACP's Missouri branch, told The Kansas City Star.
“We’ve never received such frequent complaints.”
This is the first such warning that the organization has issued about the U.S. state.
The group cited events such as racist insults towards black students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tory Sanders, a 28-year-old black from Tennessee.
Sanders died earlier this year under suspicious circumstances after running out of gasoline while driving through the state and was taken into custody by Missouri police without being charged with the crime.
In addition, the note refers to a recently drafted report by the Attorney General of Missouri, which shows that in the state, black drivers' cars are 75% more likely to be stopped than those of whites.
"The remark is designed for people to be aware of their families, friends and colleagues what might happen in Missouri," Chapel said.
"People need to be prepared, so that's the bail money or informing relatives that they're going through the state."
Based on latest data from the FBI's hate crime reporting program, 100 hate crimes were documented in Missouri in 2015, placing the state's 16th place in a cross-section in terms of similar violations.
A travel warning is also in response to Missouri's new law, which makes it more difficult for a company to sue for discrimination in search of housing or work.
The American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) had previously issued travel remarks about Texas and Arizona after these states passed immigration laws that required local abilities to arrest people for immigration-related violations because, according to ACLU, it increases racial profiling.
Typically, travel warnings are issued by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs on other countries, but recently, interest groups have started to use this measure in response to certain laws and trends within the United States.
Hepatitis: what could everyone know about the different forms of this serious disease?
Hepatitis or inflammation of the liver can be caused by five types of hepatitis A, B, C, D and E viruses.
All these viruses provoke acute inflammation of liver tissue.
However, chronic hepatitis B and C has a malignant course, which can develop into liver cirrhosis, i.e. wrinkling of the liver and liver cancer.
Kuulo Kutsar, Epidemiology Advisor of the Health Board, says that the symptoms of acute hepatitis are the same for all types of liver inflammation - fever, tiredness, loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, grayish stools, dark urine, muscle and joint pain, and yellowing of the skin and mucous membranes.
From the last sign of the disease, the popular name for inflammation of the liver is jaundice.
Hepatitis in children may occur without symptoms.
It is possible to determine the different types of inflammation of the liver only on the basis of data from a laboratory examination.
Acute hepatitis can turn into chronic hepatitis and progress for decades without symptoms, which means that a person may not know anything about their dangerous liver-damaging disease.
Kuulo Kutsar highlights different forms of hepatitis and describes their course in more detail.
Hepatitis A viruses are spread from a sick person and infect us by eating contaminated food or drinking water, through sex, from the feces of an infected person from his hands and hands to foods, surfaces, objects, getting into your mouth, and when consuming drugs.
The risk of contracting hepatitis A includes family members and caregivers, people suffering from chronic liver diseases, people who have sex with an infected person, men having sex with men, drug addicts, travellers visiting high-spreading countries for hepatitis A.
Infection can be prevented by vaccination, in particular vaccination is recommended for people who travel heavily and are at risk of hepatitis A.
In addition, it is important to meet the daily hygiene requirements: wash or disinfect your hands, wash all fruits and vegetables before consuming food.
Hepatitis B viruses are spread by contact with the blood of a sick person, by injecting drugs, tattoos, piercings, using household items contaminated with blood (toothbrushes, shaving equipment), with donor blood, having sex with a person suspected of having hepatitis, and from a person with hepatitis from a mother to a foetus/newborn.
Injecting drug addicts, sex partners of an infected person, men having sex with men, newborns of an infected mother, sick family members and caregivers, health care workers, rescue workers and police officers in contact with blood and other bodily fluids, and travelers visiting countries with high levels of hepatitis B.
It is possible to vaccinate people for the prevention of Hepatitis B; in particular, it is recommended to vaccinate people at risk, as well as people suffering from chronic liver diseases, HIV-infected, sexually transmitted diseases and people with diabetes.
Other preventive measures for hepatitis B include: refraining from the use of foreign hygiene and manicure equipment, other sharps and syringe-needles, tattooing and piercing, and the use of a condom in the event of infected sex or avoiding sexual risk behavior.
Vaccination against hepatitis B is part of the national immunisation schedule in Estonia and is free for children.
Hepatitis C viruses are transmitted through contact with the blood of a sick person, by injection of drugs, tattoos, piercings, the use of household items contaminated with blood, donor blood, having sex with a person suspected of having hepatitis, and from a mother with hepatitis to a foetus/neonate.
Injecting drug addicts, sex partners of an infected person, men having sex with men, newborns of an infected mother, sick family members and caregivers, health care workers and police officers in contact with blood and other bodily fluids, HIV-infected persons, and travellers visiting countries with a high incidence of hepatitis C.
The preventive measure for hepatitis C is: abstainince from the use of foreign hygiene and manicure equipment, other sharps and syringe-needles, tattooing and piercings, and the use of a condom in case of infectious sex.
There is no vaccine against hepatitis C.
Hepatitis D is caused by the Hepatitis D virus, which multiplies in the presence of the hepatitis B virus.
As a result, Hepatitis D infection occurs together with hepatitis B or as a superinfection in patients with chronic hepatitis B.
Hepatitis D viruses are transmitted during contact with blood and other body fluids, with syringe-needles contaminated with them, sexually and rarely from an infected mother to the foetus.
The risk group for hepatitis D is chronic carriers of the hepatitis B virus and people who do not have immunity against hepatitis B.
An effective preventive measure is vaccination with the hepatitis B vaccine, which also protects a person from infection with the hepatitis D virus.
Hepatitis E viruses are transmitted by faecal-oral drinking water and food, including low-cooked beef, fish and clams, donor blood, shared syringes, and an infected mother to the foetus.
Travelers can become infected with hepatitis E in countries with a high incidence.
The preventive measures against hepatitis E are compliance with hygiene requirements: wash or disinfect your hands after going to the toilet, before cooking and eating, after caring for the patient.
Wash fruits and vegetables before consumption, avoid eating low-cooked beef, fish and seafood, drink only safe drinking water and refrain from drug use.
Although in the last round round the competitors tried to push the four-time Olympic champion off the track, the 34-year-old Farah found strength in the final stretch and defeated with a time of 26.49.51.
Farah didn't think long, dragging with his wife Tania and their children after the finish.
It was a special moment for me.
I miss my family very much.
Being on the track with them was wonderful," Farah told reporters.
Farah is aiming for the fifth title race in London in a row with a golden double of 5,000 and 10 000 metres.
Anything is possible if you believe in it.
Despite the title, Anne-Marie O'Connor's "The Lady in Gold: The Extraordinary Story of the Portrait of Gustav Klimt's Masterpiece by Adele Bloch-Bauer" dives much deeper from the birth of a work of art into the cultural life of Vienna at the end of the 19th and early 20th centuries.
Even more thoroughly in history.
The awe-inspiring amount of information might have benefited from a harder axe, a narrower topic would have allowed the extraordinary story of the portrait allowed in the title to gain more influence.
At the moment, there would be two books: Gustav Klimt and Adele Bloch-Bauer's story depicted in his painting "The Lady in Gold", and a much larger and more comprehensive history book about esteemed Jewish families and the German occupation with its aftermath in Austria.
The first third of the book leads to the cultural life of Vienna in the middles, and those who found themselves in the Austrian capital can effortlessly imagine how more than a hundred years ago life went around Ringstrasse, or how everyone turned their heaads in the central café when Klimt walked in.
The concentration of cultural figures was high in one of Europe's riciest cities, and when you saw the familiar name in the book, you feel like a gram more cultivated.
At the same time, for example, the fact that Sigmund Freud acted in a city where incurable syphilis proliferated and was the highest level of suicide in Europe, seems to add nothing to the development of the story.
Or perhaps, every brushstroke of a few sentences also adds colors to the cultural life of the beginning of the 20th century in Vienna.
A dream of a trip to the Soviet Union
In the last decades of the 19th-century, the number of Jews in Vienna had exploded, becoming the largest Jewish community in Western Europe.
At the turn of the century, almost one in tenth Viennese was Jewish.
Adele's parents were also newcomers in Vienna, but by the time Adele married the opening of the Czech sugar magnate Ferdinand Bloch twice and posing for Klimt's famous paintings, his father's bank had already become the seventh largest bank in the Habsburg Empire.
But Adelet didn't smoke all the glamorous life she belonged to.
If you look at "The Lady's in Gold", it is hard to believe that towards the end of his life, this noble lady was infected with socialism and dreamed of a trip to the Soviet Union.
By 1903, when Ferdinand Bloch decided to order a portrait of her husband from Klimt, the hottest painter in Vienna at the last moment.
Klimt's order at that time cost 4000 kroons, a quarter of the price of a country house with a good location.
The fact that in some portraits the master's love affair with the model was suspected did not deter sugar magnate.
As for Adele, the only definite fact is that the close relationship between the model and the artist that emerged in the course of the work continued for the rest of her life.
Anne-Marie O'Connor - "The Lady in Gold.
An extraordinary story by Gustav Klimt's master's work, Adele Bloch-Bauer's portrait", Varrak 2016, translated from English by Aldo Randmaa.
Immediately before the beginning of his work with Adele, Klimt had been to Ravenna in San Vitale Basilica, and the researchers think that the glorious mosaics seened there from Empress Theodora gave the artist the idea to make a golden portrait of Adele as a painted mosaic.
Klimt presented his "Lady in Gold" in Vienna in 1908, and around the same time (1907-1908), another golden painting "Kiss" was born.
Art historians did not miss the similarity between the two women.
What does Hitler have to do with the story?
"I think to remember my friend Klimt, I owe him money to make his handiwork available to the public," Adele Bloch-Bauer wrote a few years after the artist's departure (Klimt died in February 1918) to the director of the Belvedere Museum and wanted their family's Klimt's collection to go to the museum.
Six years before his death, Klimt produced another portrait of Adele.
Also in his will (Adele left in February 1925), he asked her husband to leave her two portraits and four Gustav Klimt's landscape paintings to the Austrian Gallery.
She bequeathed her jewelry to her nieces Maria and Luis.
A year after Klimt had graduated "Austria's Mona Lisa", 17-year-old Adolf Hitler had tried to get into the Vienna Academy of Fine Arts, but failed at a drawing exam.
While Hitler's later anti-Semitism is sometimes attributed to the fact that Jewish professors did not accept him into the academy, none of his examiners were actually Jewish.
In any case, the task of the Austrian Gallery referred to in Maria's Testament had changed considerably by 1939 - it had become important to find works to please the Nazi Party from Vienna's rich collections of Jewish art.
Some of Adele's works at home, but not Klimt's, were also suitable for Hitler's personal art repository.
Sixty years after Nazi art robberies, a ten-year litigation between the Austrian government and Bloch-Bauer heirs began.
All that's noted here is that while "Kiss" can be seen at Belvedere Castle in Vienna, "Lady's Gold" depicting Adele Bloch-Bauer must be seen in the Neue Galeries of New York.
And before the trip to Vienna, read the aforementioned book.
Even if you don't plan to see Klimt's works, because at least my emotions revolved around the Nazi and Jewish theme more after the book was closed than a painting sold for 135 million dollars and model Adele Bloch-Bauer.
Therefore, not so much a portrait, but rather a story of the fate of the family - and a very touching story.
Significantly facilitated orienteering in the events was facilitated by the family tree of the Bauers mentioned at the beginning of the book.
A 17-year-old swimmer was very close to an Estonian record in Canada
In pre-swimming, Gold was very close to his 200 m backstroking personal record of 2.16.62.
The top mark dates back to a 17-year-old girl a week ago, when the Canadian junior championships became 2.16.58, according to Priit Aavik's Swimming Blog.
Gold made it to the A-finals in the tenth.
In the finale, he completed the distance with a time of 2.15.76 and achieved a good seventh place with a new powerful top brand.
From Alina Kendzior's Estonian record (2.15.13), Gold was separated by a little more than half a second.
Gold came third in Estonia's all-time rankings and his fresh personal top mark is Estonia's all-time fifth result.
A young hope for the future is still starting at the Canadian Adult Championships in 100 m, 200 m and 400 m freestroke and 200 m and 400 m complex swimming.
Last week took place at the Canadian junior championships in Toronto, and Gold made successful starts there as well.
He won a silver medal with a time of 2.03.22.
This distance was most recently swoven by an Estonian woman in 2008.
Only Elina Partõka, an Estonian record woman, has swam this distance faster than Gold in Estonia.
An Estonian woman living in Canada also won a bronze medal in the 100 m freestyle with an excellent personal record and a top mark of the new Estonian season of 56.66.
Kertu Ly Alnek, who is a year older than him, has swam this distance only one century faster.
She reached her personal top mark in another 100 m backstroke, receiving a time of 1.03.69 in the opening exchange of the relay.
It was only the 7th place in the distance for half when he swam 2.17.31 in the final.
Gold is currently leading the Estonian season rankings in four different distances.
President Trump goes on vacation, critics sharpen their teeth
It's time for vacations - it also means relaxing for US President Donald Trump.
However, as Trump has so far been one of the president's most vacation days, critics are already sharpening their teeth.
According to U.S. Today and CNN, the president's plan is to spend a 17-day holiday at a self-named golf club in Bedminster, New Jersey.
Even though the president's holiday began yesterday, Trump's opponents have already spoken out for a week.
They're reproaching Trump for resting too often.
Namely, it's the president's practice to spend time on weekends in the villas in Florida, Virginia and New Jersey.
By the way, Trump is not the most rested president in his 196 days.
For example, George W. Bush managed to be on vacation for 67 days in that time, while Barack Obama was half as much as Trump, just 21 days.
However, things tend to be quickly forgotten by Trump.
A few days after becoming president, Trump said "60 Minutes" that there won't be any time for long vacations during his presidency, as there's very much to be done.
Three years ago he also expressed outrage about Barack Obama's holiday.
"While Obama's on vacation while Obama's on vacation, playing golf, attending parties and jazz concerts, ISIS chopping his heads off for journalists."
According to White House officials, a 17-day visit to a golf club is not exactly a kind of relaxation.
Instead, it's officially called a business holiday, during which Trump doesn't have time for reading or other favorite activities.
In addition, there had to be another reason for the working leave.
Namely, an air conditioning system will be replaced in the west wing of the White House, which is why all personnel had to leave.
There is also an Oval Cabinet and other presidential offices in the western wing.
"I don't think any of you would want to be in the western wing in August if there's 38 degrees and there's no air conditioner," White House spokesman Lindsay Walters said, stressing that the president would continue to work while on vacation.
You'll get an idea of where President Trump's going to spend the next 17 days.
Blocking critics from Facebook: Don't run for public office if you don't tolerate criticism from voters
The father of our country knew a lot about unfavorable media coverage.
The Americans loved George Washington, but it didn't take long for newspapers to start beating him for everything from domestic politics to his political principles.
Criticism, of course, irritated him.
But he didn't force his critics to remain silent.
Because in 1783, Washington saided that "free speech can be deprived of us, and mutely and silently, we can be led to massacres like sheep."
It brings me to Maryland Governor Larry Hogan, who's gonna have to work hard to be more like Washington.
Hogan's subordinates have blocked and deleted the posts of at least 450 people, who published their opinions on his official Facebook page.
And towards the beginning of the week, the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) sued him.
The governor's officials stamped the lawsuit, and online commentaries were full of counter-reactions in the style of "Who's interested?".
"It's just Facebook," many said.
But it counts.
And it counts a lot when it comes to a man like Hogan.
It is an astonishingly popular Republican governor in an overwhelmingly democratic state.
His approval rate ranks second among the country's 50 governors.
Hogan's not a mind-blinding responsive head.
He has led the state at a steady time and remained strictly true to his principles.
In addition, he has used Facebook as the main tool when communicating with his constituents, for example, presenting his bald head cheerfully for the first time after chemotherapy.
Thus, the governor's page  is an officially labeled public forum, managed by staff who receive pay from taxpayers' dollars  blocking people visiting is unnecessary and, ultimately, dangerous.
Hogani's spokeswoman Amelia Chasse defended the governor's behavior in an interview with The Washington Post, arguing that blocking the comments was nothing more than moderation.
But it is too easy to paint a picture of internet trolls, spammers or people full of anger.
The Post had a conversation with several real people that Hogan blocked.
And that's exactly what they are  real people who communicate with their elected managers. Teacher, business owner and pastor, not internet trolls.
They all said their comments were respectful, thoughtful, and by no means obscene.
In his post, the pastor quoted the Bible, emphasizing Hogan's Catholicism.
Lakshmi Sarma Ramani, a lawyer from Bowie, Maryland, did not incite anger, but on real hate crimes.
“I politely commented that I was disappointed because he hasn’t reacted to hate crimes or other recent news,” he wrote in a comment section of The Post.
"I also don't like the idea that when a lot of people comment on the same issue, some immediately call it an indifferently collective effort, instead of admitting that they are dealing with a large number of worried citizens."
What the governor's office called a "directed spam attack" would probably be called "public support" by others.
The Facebook erage makes it easy to match messages by simply blocking a critic or deleting a negative comment.
It's much cleaner than it used to be when, in order to do the same, employees had to be expelled so that they would collect newspapers with critical editorials and burn them or arrest the protesters and force them to remain silent.
But that's exactly what's going on right now, just digital.
Hogan isn't the first government official to be criticized for killing Facebook and other social media channels.
President Trump has sued Twitter users who have been blocked from his Twitter feed.
One of the first landmarked verdicts on the subject was made last week in Virginia.
U.S. District Judge James C. Cacheris ruled in Alexandria that Loudoun's county supervisory board chairwoman violated the first amendment to the constitution when he blocked the voter from his Facebook page.
In Kentucky, ACLU also visited Governor Matt Bevin (R) for using Facebook and Twitter.
It shouldn't be that hard.
In the time of Washington, affordable postage had a similar effect as the internet.
Between 1776 and 1800 quadrupled the number of newspapers and anonymous letterwriters beaten his manner of leadership.
And even then, Washington had to face anonymous trolleybuses.
People wrote letters to newspapers under such pseudonyms as Juricola, Valerius, Belisarius, and Portius, destroying Washington's decisions.
According to historical documents collected as part of an online George Washington mail-collection project, he was buried in the cabinet under petitions that criticized his positions regarding friendship, trade, and shipping agreements with the United Kingdom.
But he didn't force them to remain silent.
Freedom of speech, dissenting opinions and debates are the foundations of our country.
Proper management means accepting it.
Ronan Rafferty counts on the comfort of home territory at Renaissance Club
Former European number one Ronan Rafferty hopes for the comfort of home territory over the next three days when the 25th Scottish Senior Open takes place at East Lothian's Renaissance Club golf courses.
The Northern Irian, who was in charge last year ahead of the last round in the neighboring Archerfield Links last year before his surrender to Paul Eales, has become affectionate for the new venue and will play the role of an ambassador for the tournament instead of the club's founder and CEO Jerry Sarvad.
“I saw Renaissance Club built, and now we’re going to shine the club in all its glory with this event,” Rafferty said.
"Players can see the path at their best, it's in glorious condition.
Jerry has done an excellent job of building this place.
He is an enthusiastic supporter of the golf game and is proud of what he has achieved here.
This makes it possible to present the trail perfectly."
Eales is looking forward that he will be able to defend the championship for the first time, even though he has won both the European Tour and the European Challenge Tour during his 32-year career.
"This is accompanied by a very special feeling," said the player in Southport.
"I couldn't defend my Extremadura Open title because it was taken off the competition calendar a year after my victory.
It's magical to come back to this neighborhood.
It's a great place to play golf.
I remember the memories of last year, for me and my wife Sharon, last year's victory was special.
It was a very special time that we will always wear in our hearts.”
Gary Orr, who joined the Senior European tour towards the beginning of this year when he turned 50, will also return to competition.
This will be his first event in Scotland since taking part in Aberdeen Asset Management Scottish Open in 2013.
"Playing at home is always a little more meaningful," said Orr, Helensburch man.
"You want to perform well, but at the same time you also feel additional tension.
I've enjoyed playing again immensely and shown quite a decent game of golf.
I'm happy with the progress so far."
Ian Woosnam, a former Masters winner, and Paul Broadhurst, Roger Chapman, Mark James and Mark McNulty will join the trio.
Orr and former Ryder Cup captain Sam Torrance are two of the six Scots, the rest are Andrew Oldcorn, Stephen McAllister, Bill Longmuir and Ross Drummond.
Entrance is free, and car parking costs 5 pounds for a car with one passenger and 10 pounds for a car with two or more passengers.
All proceeds go to the charity Prostate Cancer UK.
Poverty and Death in Indonesia's Gold Land
When Bardina Degei prepares dinner, she won't use the stove.
He rarely uses a pot.
In a wooden cabin in Enarotal, the capital of Paniai Regenty in Indonesia's stubborn province of Papua, the housewife places a notated sweet potato in the local language directly on the fireplace.
After half an hour, the charred tuber is taken from the heat and eagerly put into the skin with unwashed hands.
Degei sits on a muddy floor  there is no furniture ≥ and at the same time he also does his daily household chores, for example, washes laundry with cloudy water from nearby swamps.
The role of the toilet is performed by a bucket in a room without a roof.
Since she is the youngest of her husband's four wives, she's not destined to take care of any fields.
Polygamy is common here.
Working late, however, can be dangerous ♖ Most of the men in the village are unemployed, and many of them drink a lot, and there are also soldiers here.
"No one dares to visit after five o'clock in the evening," he says.
This is a rare glimpse into life in Papua's Highlands, a former Dutch colony annexed to Indonesia in 1969 after a controversial referendum, during which only 1026 olders were forced to vote in front of the occupier's forces.
The movement that prompted the liberation of Dutch supremacy quickly directed its ravage against Jakarta's government, which continues to keep the region under its strong control, keeping away foreign journalists and human rights supervisors.
In 2003, the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua, so that the eastern part of the island remained in the independent Papua New Guinea.
Enarotali is both a remote and an abandoned place. To get here, you need to fly from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire for 90 minutes and then take a naughty five-hour journey with a rental car.
There is no public transport.
With a population of about 19 000, the city consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences and covered with wavy iron roofs, the rust of which has been turned into yellow-browns of different shades.
Very few Indonesians have made the journey here, not to mention journalists, and there have hardly been any foreigners.
Before the arrival of Christian missions, Mee Pago's papuas worshipped a god named Uga Tamee.
There were also other changes.
"We weren't used to wearing clothes like that," says Degei, referring to his colorful hand-woven turban, dark blouse and bright skirt.
"Before that, we only wore plant leaves on the body."
Papua is the poorest province in Indonesia, with 28% of people living below the poverty line, and infant mortality and illiteracy statistics are one of the worst in Asia.
But it's also Indonesia's golden country.
Just 100 km from the Pania, a mountainous provincie of approximately 153,000 people in New Jersey, lies the world's largest and most lucrative gold mine Grasberg, which is owned by Freeport McMoran in Phoenix.
In 2015 alone, Freeport extracted around US$3.1 billion worth of gold and copper.
In addition, Papua can boast of consumer timber reserves worth an estimated US$78 billion.
However, these riches are a source of Papuate's suffering, because they give rise to a powerful suffocating presence of the Indonesian army.
A 2005 study by The New York Times found that Freeport paid approximately US$20 million to local military and military units between 1998 and 2004, including up to US$150,000 for one officer.
Papuates' efforts to ensure greater autonomy threaten this golden goose and are ruthlessly reluctantly handled.
According to human rights activists, more than 500 000 papuas have been killed since 1969, and thousands more have been raped, tortured and jailed by the Indonesian military.
According to the Asian Human Rights Commission, there was a mass killing of genocide in Papua's tribal highlands in the 1970s.
Last year alone, more than 3,900 peaceful protesters were arrested by Indonesian police in the area.
Birsbane Archdiocese's 2016 report "We Lose Everything" contains testimonies of the atrocities committed in the previous year, such as extrajudicial killings, torture (according to another report, rape and giving electric shocks are especially prevalent) and the brutal suppression of peaceful demonstrations.
"It's hard to count the number of casualties, because there are such incidents every week," says Andreas Harsono, an Indonesian researcher at Human Rights Watch.
Screws are even tighter screwed down, because Papua's natural resources have caused an influx by new settlers from other parts of Indonesia.
83% of the province's 3.5 million inhabitants are Christians, but demography is changing as Muslim economic migrants arrive from Indonesia's populous Java, Kalimantani, Sumatra and Sulawesi islands.
Javan family canteens sell gado-gado with crispy chicken and mixed vegetables, which are served with peanut sauce.
The locals can't compete with them.
"Migrants began to sell chicken and vegetables in the traditional market cheaper than local papuas," explains 24-year-old Abeth You, who was born in Paniai, who moved to Jayapura, the provincial capital, for work.
'Indigenous papuas  papua mama-mama [women]  will therefore lose their market share."
Indonesian President Joko Widodo, who is popularly known as Jokowi, pledged in his 2014 election campaign to deal with these inequalities and human rights violations in Papua.
The former carpenter defeated 27 out of 29 constituencies, including Paniai, on his way to Jakarta's Presidential Palace.
But there's very little changed in Papua, and the locals feel deceived.
"Our hearts have been broken because in 2014 we voted for Jokow in anticipation that he would fulfill our hopes regarding the restoration of justice," says You.
In fact, Paniai was in a slump just two months after Jokow took office in October.
On December 7, 2014, 11 children sang Christmas songs at the Enarotalis campfire, when two Indonesian soldiers appeared on a motorcycle from the darkness.
Frightened children told them they should turn on the headlights.
According to local pastor Yavedt Tebai, one of the soldiers was offended and later returned with four soldiers.
Victims and eyewitnesses said drunk soldiers chased the children and beaten them with their rifle handles.
Then one of the soldiers shot into the children.
One child, 16-year-old Yulianus Yeimot, was beaten so cruelly that he fell into a coma.
A few hours later, the nearby electoral commission building was set on fire, and things escalated further the next day.
About 1,000 young Papuan men, women, and children gathered on a football field in front of a local police station and a military command center to demand justice.
They wore ceremonial hunting bows and performed waita-dance of the Papua honey pago tribe, during which they runned around and imitated birdsong.
Some protesters began throwing stones at the police and the military.
"When an outrage flared up, soldiers were sent commands via internal radio. "If the crowd resists more than three times, shoot them," according to an official document, which has been seen by TIME magazine, but which has not been issued to local media outlets.
Yeremias Kayame (56), the elder of Kego Koto district in the city of Enarotal, felt an imminent threat and urged to stay calm, asking the crowd to go home.
No one wanted to listen.
"When I turned around, I was suddenly shotered to my left wrist," he told TIME magazine on the porch of a wooden house in bright colors.
Kayame still doesn't know who the shooter was, but he says the bullet came from the ranks of assembled soldiers.
"There were a lot of people, there were plenty of shots," he adds.
Alfius Youw was hit three times, according to a cousin who witnessed his shooting.
"I ran to him and examined his body to make sure it's him," Yohanes told TIME, using only one name like many Indonesians.
"I saw that he's dead... I kissed him."
Papua Police Chief Inspector Yotje Mende told reporters that his subordinates only ensured the safety of his precinct because it was attacked.
"We must defend ourselves if we are threatened with kill," spokeswoman Pudjo Sulistiyo, a spokeswoman for Papua police in 2015.
"It's a matter of life and death."
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters were killed and there were many injuries.
News about the killings didn't reach Jakarta until the next day.
Three weeks later, Jokowi gave a passionate speech in Jayapura, where he expressed condolences to the families of the victims and pledged to engage in abuse in Papua.
"I want to solve this case right away, so it won't happen again in the future," he said.
Security Minister Wiranto said in October 2016 that he would introduce an out-of-court settlement mechanism to solve crimes against human rights.
Apologies followed almost immediately.
"Most crimes happened a long time ago.
Some of them took place in the 1990s and early 2000s.
It's because we're committed to solving these crimes, but we have to follow certain processes," he said.
Speaking to TIME magazine on June 5 in Jakarta, Wiranto withdrew his lyrics, saying he had no intention of creating a mechanism in Papua to deal with complaints.
Instead, he said that "everything will be resolved according to the law."
Wiranto, who has been accused by the U.N. of crimes against human rights over the deaths of more than 1,000 persons in East Timor during the 1999 bloody independence referendum, said 11 crimes against human rights have already been solved in Papua, including what has happened in Pania.
Families of Paniai's victims accepted such claims with gloomy distrust.
"I've been interrogated four times in the last three years, but there's been no progress," Yohanes said.
"I'm tired."
He says he's still living in fear years later.
"I'm afraid," he says.
"I'm afraid the army will arrest me, I'm afraid I'm going to be shot."
His brother Yacobus insisted that Paniai's people are afraid to dissect this incident.
He said he got beaten by the military after he had helped bury four victims.
"After burying the bodies, the army came looking for me," he says.
The shootings haven't ceased.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police fired villagers in Deiyai, a neighboring Regent of Paniai.
One person was killed and 17 wounded, including children, when he went into an altercation between the villagers and the manager of the construction company, who refused to transport the unconscious man to the hospital.
This man, 24-year-old Ravianus Douw, who sank fishing in a nearby river, died on his way to the hospital.
Angry villagers protested in front of the company's office on the construction site.
According to the police, the villagers were throwing stones at the officers, to which the latter responded with warning shots.
But according to locals, the mobile brigade (Indonesian military police) began shooting at the people, killing one person.
"We were very panicked, we're afraid of vengeance," TIME's 29-year-old Dominggu Badii, who lives near the hospital and witnessed the injunctions.
"I've been hiding in my house for two days."
Deiyai's parliament has demanded the prosecution of related police officers and withdrew the police mobile brigade from the area.
Paniai has always been a problematic place for the Indonesian government.
The lack of meaningful development feed the discontent of the honey, moni, dani and Damal tribes living in Papua's louding midfield.
Many of them have joined the Free Papua Movement (VPL), an army of rebels that says it protects the rights of papuans, randomly carrying out attacks and kidnapping raids among Indonesian soldiers.
Some of the leaders of VPL are from the Paniah, including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya.
In response, under the pretext of "defensing the sovereignty of the state", the military has arrested Paniais in recent years and arbitrarily detained thousands of people.
Some of them will never appear again.
Among the people of Papua, Panias are known as the "tragical forgotten place".
Poverty feed discontent.
The lack of rice sold in Enarotalis is too expensive to buy for the locals.
Bread is just as unavailable.
People grow all their food themselves  mainly sweet potatoes, plus a little fruit and leafy vegetables.
Farming is the work of women, each of whom can maintain four or five sweet potato fields.
They usually keep most of the harvest for the family, the rest are selling in the local market.
Ten sweet potatoes cost only 10 000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, there has been an increase in economic inequality between the indigenous Papuans and the new migrants, who have arrived since the opening of new routes at Nabire airport.
The few jobs that exist go better to educated and wealthier migrants.
Papuates themselves often lack capital or the necessary skills to conduct a competitive business.
"Young people aren't interested in staying visiting because there's no jobs or money here," says John Gobai, chairman of the Council of the Paniai Tribes.
Separation keeps the world's eyes away from Papua.
In addition, there will be severe restrictions on foreign media.
Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp were expelled from Papua earlier this year because "they didn't follow relevant practices," even though they had been granted a rare filming license.
Phelim Kine, deputy head of the Asian department at Human Rights Watch, said Jokow's election promises had not been implemented to remove the restrictions on reports to promote transparency and development.
"In Indonesia, journalists trying to make reports from Papua's easternmost turbulent provinces and Western Papua are facing new threats of visa rejection and blacklisting," he said in a statement.
The lack of a drilling gaze in journalism means that the international pressure on the Indonesian government is largely limited to Papua's close neighbours.
In March, the Six Pacific states – Tonga, Nauru, Belau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands, urged the United Nations Human Rights Council to investigate “a variety of widespread crimes” in Papua, including the shooting of Paniai.
These countries have historically supported the VPL.
Arrmanatha Nasir, a spokeswoman for Indonesia's foreign ministry, dismissed the group's allegations, Jakartas told reporters: "There is still democratic order and press freedom in Indonesia, so it would be difficult to bury evidence in anti-human rights cases."
Local people want more foreign governments to be noticed.
When an official Dutch delegation, led by the country's human rights ambassador Kees Van Bar, visited Jayapura on May 4, local people interrupted their silence, according to a source who had attended the meeting but wished to remain anonymous, urging: "We want freedom."
There will be new presidential elections in Indonesia in 2019, but Papuas don't think they will vote for Jokow again.
"Jokowi is a man of good intentions, but he is surrounded by people who are involved in the shooting of Paniai," says Hobai, chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Jokow to understand that, in addition to the fact that the people of Paniai are constantly living under the threatening threat of a predatory army, they suffer misery because of poor educational and health services.
According to Gobai, the people of Paniai, like the other Papuas, consider the vote given to their Jokow to be a debt, which he will have to pay.
"They don't need money, they want justice," he says.
Despite threats and intimidation, the families of the shooting victims of Paniai managed the last symbolic rebellion ≖ they buried the body of one of the victims in the land opposite the police station and the military base.
Knowinginging that justice may never be put on its feet, at least they won't let those responsible forget their crimes.
"One of our family members was killed," Yacobus says with a lowered head.
"What else can we do?"
The fire swallowed the Dubai Torch skyscraper for the second time
The fire crashed through one of the world's tallest residential towers in Dubai on Friday, forcing hundreds of residents to escapt from this 337-meter-high and 79-storey building.
It's the second fire that's ravaged a high-rise in just two years called the Torch Tower.
The building was evacuated, no injuries were reported and no direct cause of the fire was reported.
"We slept and woke up with a fire alarm and people screaming.
We ran down the stairs and it took us about 10 minutes to get down from the 50th floor," a resident told Reuters, who called himself George.
According to Dubai's civil protection officials, firefighters extinguished the flame at about 4 a.m. local time (00.00.00 GMT) and cooled the building.
The government said they were working to provide shelter for victims.
This incident could once again raise questions about the safety of materials used on the outside of high-rise buildings around the world.
An investigation carried out by the Torch Board after the fire in 2015 found that the most damage was suffered by the façade coating - the decorative panels and insulation of the outer surface.
British police said they believe London's Grenfell Tower insulation and facade panels may have contributed to the rapid spread of the June fire, which killed about 80 people.
See: These phones will probably become bestsellers in the autumn
Spring and Autumn is a sweet time for tech fans, because then phone manufacturers will unveil their new top models.
Samsung Galaxy S8, Huawei P10 and Sony Xperia XZ Premium went on sale in the spring, and new hit phones are also expected on store shelves in the autumn.
According to Tele2's marketing and sales director Kristjan Seema, this year's flagships are characterized by an innovative design, an iris scanner, an edge-to-edge 4K resolution display and an ultra-fast processor.
The Samsung Galaxy S8, which has also reached the top of Estonia's smartphone sales rankings, created great excitement in the world of technology.
Autumn allows smart enthusiasts even more agile fast charging, a more durable battery, a higher quality camera and higher water and dust resistance.
He addeded that based on rumors, it is already possible to make assumptions and predict four top models that will probably conquer this year's sales rankings.
These fould top models of smartphones will arrive on store shelves in the autumn of this year.
The long-awaited iPhone 8 and iPhone 8 Plus are expected to hit the shelves as early as mid-Septe
Rumors about what a new Apple model will look like and what you can do with it will spread a lot.
While the iPhone 7 didn't make a significant difference next to the iPhone 6s, for Apple's tenth birthday, the new model is expected to stand out completely from the previous ones.
There are four colors spoken: black, white, rose gold and chrome.
One of the elements of the new design is probably a screen that covers the entire front panel of the phone, a home button has been lost, and the fingerprint reader is built into the screen.
JonyIve, Apple's design manager, insists that the new iPhone model looks like smooth glass.
There has also been talk about the ceramic version of the iPhone.
In addition to an iris scanner, wireless charging and an even higher-quality camera, a faster A11 processor can be expected.
It's definitely one of the most anticipated smartphones of the year.
Pre-ordering of the all-new Galaxy Note 8 will start on August 23.
There have been a lot of rumours of what the new Galaxy Note 8 will look like.
It's said it's even more designed for business users, and the S Pen allows for even greater functionalities.
The phone should have two cameras, one of which is a wide-angle and the other a telephotone with a narrower field of view.
At the moment, no one knows what two rear cameras are capable of yet, but in any case, this is Samsung's first experiment, which is certainly comparable in quality to the iPhone 8.
Let’s look forward to August 23.
While Nokia has launched three smartphones this year, led by the Nokia 6, their flagship is still missing, the Nokia 8, which will be unveiled as early as August 16.
We're looking forward to Nokia's 5.3-inch edge-to-edge display and the ultra-fast Qualcomm Snapdragon 835 processor and more durable battery.
In the case of Nokia, there is also talk of an aluminum body and a significant degree of water and dust resistance IP68.
Similarly to Samsung, Nokia's new model has two rear cameras manufactured by Zeiss.
If Sony's solid flagship has so far been the XZ Premium, the manufacturer has confirmed that at least two smartphones with even more innovative features will be added in the near future.
We are looking forward to a smartphone that is a little more modest than the XZ Premium, but at least with an equally good or better camera.
It has been speculated about Sony's new model that its screen is at least six-inches.
What the reality will bring, we will be able to see very soon, because traditionally Sony has unveiled its autumn flagship at the international technology fair IFA, which this year will take place from 1 to 6 September.
According to leaked transcripts of phone calls, Trump put pressure on Mexico to pay for the border wall
The Washington Post unveiled transcripts of phone calls between U.S. President Donald Trump and Mexican and Australian leaders.
On January 27, Trump went on a phone call at Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto to stop publicly proclaiminging that he was not going to pay for the proposed border wall.
The American president crackled with him over public condemnation of the wall, saying, "You can't say it to the press."
Trump set up his presidential campaign promise to build a wall along the southern border of the United States and swore to make Mexico pay for the project.
Transcripts from his first speech with Australian Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull have also been released, revealing a tense exchange of ideas.
Trump told Turnbull that the agreement that the United States would take on refugees going to Australia is "ridiculous," "franky," and "stupid." He claimed the deal, which was made under Obama's administration, would give him the impression of a "ducker" because he has proposed a ban on resettling refugees to the United States.
Trump ended the call, announcing to Turnbull that "this was the most unpleasant call of the day."
The Supreme Court did not discuss the appeal of OÜ MRP Linna Liinid in a case where the City of Tartu rejected their tender for the procurement of regular bus services from July 2019 to 2029.
It is a directive that rejected OÜ MRP Linna Liinid's offer in Tartu bus procurement because the offer did not meet the requirements, explained Anneli Apuhtin, Head of the Tartu City Government Justice Service.
This is a procurement for public regular services within the city of Tartu for the period 01.07.2019-30.06.2029.
MRP Linna Liinid appealed the Tartu Circuit Court's ruling to the Supreme Court.
The Supreme Court did not hear it.
Although on social media, many people are complaining that the noise from the Weekend festival is disturbing their night's sleep and the whole city is full of intoxicated partygoers, there are also many people who don't have a bad word to say about the festival that lasts on the beach.
I'm even positively surprised that the partygoers in the store were so polite!
I didn't see any blurredness or rudeness.
Even I saw kindness," says Laine.
"It's nice that people come, be and enjoy Pärnu, which is like hibernating in winter," he adds cheerfully.
Laine's sleep doesn't bother the beacfall.
