Last summer there was the other stage, then we heard more.
We live outside the city ourselves, that we can actually sound quite well there far by the Pärnu River," he says.
Pearson cuts another 3,000 jobs and cuts dividends to revive the business
Pearson, a British education company, said on Friday that it would cut another 3,000 jobs and reduce its interim dividends by 72 percent, triggering another restructuring programme to solve the distress that threatens the business.
According to CEO John Fallon, the jobs to be lost, which are likely to be laid off at the end of 2018 and the beginning of 2019, will be added to the 4,000 jobs lost in the course of the previous restructuring.
Pearson announced a plan to cut costs by another 300 million pounds (394 million US dollars) as part of a third-cost reduction program in May to try to revive a business that has been hit by a rapid transition to digital learning.
The company, which agreed to sell a stake in Penguin Random House last month to its partner company Bertelsmann, said their expectation over the year has not changed since sales revenue growth of 1.05 billion pounds was announced in the first half of the year.
On Sunday, the warm summer weather will continue
In Estonia, the summer weather with the possibility of thunderstorms with raisers arrives on Sunday, wind gusts are sometimes very strong.
At night there will be cloudy clearing weather in Estonia.
In many places it rains, there may be thunderstorms.
Blowing southwesterly and westerly winds 5-11, in gusts up to 14, on islands and the coasture up to 18 m/s.
The air temperature is 13-17 degrees.
The day will come with intermittent cloudy weather.
In many places it rains, in places it is possible thunderstorms.
Blowing southwesterly wind 6-12, gusts up to 15, up to 20 m/s on islands and coastures.
The air temperature is 18-21 degrees.
Convoy Attack in Kabul: A NATO Soldier Killed in Suicidal Attack
According to officials, a suicide bomber attacked a military convoy in Kabul, the capital of Afghanistan, killing one NATO soldier and wounding several others.
A NATO statement said five soldiers and an interpreter were injured.
Uncertained local reports said three civilians were killed in an incident in the city's Qarabagh district.
This bombardment is the last of an act of violence that has hit Afghanistan this month - more than 30 people were killed in a Herat mosque attack on Tuesday.
On Monday, a suicide bomber killed two people in Kabul, near the Iraqi embassy.
A NATO statement said that Thursday night's explosion occurred when a joint patrol with the Afghan army was attacked with a "human-carried self-made explosive device."
According to the statement, everything injured is in a stable condition and is being treated at Bagram Airport, a U.S. military hospital.
Correspondents said the Taliban and the group, known as the so-called Islamic State (ISIS), a growing force in Qarabagh is a serious source of concern for the forces stationed in NATO's Bagram.
According to the United Nations, at least 1662 civilians have been killed in Afghanistan in the first half of 2017.
At the end of July, a car bomb in Kabul killed at least 30 people mainly in the Shiite district.
According to the Taliban, they were behind the attack.
On May 31, a major bomb attack killed more than 150 people in the city centre, which has resulted in the most fatal military attack in the country since U.S.-led forces displaced the Taliban from power in 2001.
This violence highlights the uncertain security situation in Afghanistan, while U.S. President Donald Trump is considering whether to increase the number of U.S. forces helping the country's military and police.
Correspondents say suicide bombings in Kabul in recent weeks have also led to a public crisis of confidence in the government, its policies, and especially President Ashraf Ghani.
EPA overturns decision to depose sludging order after actions
The United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) overturned a decision to delay the imposition of an Obama-era decree requiring states to smuggle emissions, one day after 15 states sued the agency as a result of this move.
The EPA announced its decision to proceed with "2015 ozone-determination operations" late Wednesday, saying it demonstrates the Agency's commitment to working with the states.
"We believe that we need to have a dialogue with and listen to our partners from our states," EPA chief Scott Pruitt said in a statement.
Pruitt had announced in June the EPA's intention to postpone ozone determinations, which measures smog pollution in different parts of the country in order to determine where its level should be reduced by one year to October 2018 in order to meet a stricter air quality standard.
15 primarily democratic states filed a lawsuit with the District of Columbia on Tuesday, saying the decision was illegal.
President Donald Trump's government has been trying to pull back a number of environmental regulations imposed by former President Barack Obama, which Trump said is part of a more comprehensive plan to boost economic growth.
However, these aspirations have provoked reactions among Democrats and nature conservation groups, who are concerned about the effects of increased pollutants on human health.
According to the EPA website, inhalation of ozone-containing air "may reduce lung performance and increase the risk of respiratory diseases," such as asthma.
There may be a party in German pigs.
There could be a party for German pigs and cattle as heavy rains threaten the harvest of Europe's second-largest wheat producer, Bloomberg reports.
This year, it may happen in such a way that weeks-long precipitations before harvesting may mean that the fruit intended for the bread industry is suitable only for feeding to animals.
"Wheat quality is far worse than last year and worse than in 2015," said Bernhard Chilla, an analyst at Agravis Raiffeisen.
Up to 40 percent of German wheat can go into animal feed this year, which is double compared to the usual, Agravis predicted.
German farmers, like their European counterparts, have been exposed to all kinds of weather this year.
First, the winter was too dry.
Then came a cold spring, which in June was replaced by a heatwave.
In July, Germany was hit by precipitations, which have also continued in August.
The eyes of many are turned to northern Germany, where the rain prevents harvesting.
So far, only five percent of wheat has been harvested.
Most of the regions have not yet harvestered, the local collector Ceravis reported.
"It's pretty difficult days," said Frank Deckert from Rendsburg, a senior trader at Ceravis.
Consistency in everything, i.e. cunning quirks in the interior design of Finns
