Mikey Garcia and Vasyl Lomachenko meet for the first time, discuss possible confrontation
Vasyl Lomachenko and Mikey Garcia, who are supposed to be on a collision course to face each other in the closest boxing match of up to 66.7 kg, met for the first time on Thursday.
They were in separate rooms at the ESPN's office in Los Angeles until Garcia, an undefeated World Boxing Council lightweight champion, walked to the other end of the corridor to greet Lomachenko, World Boxing Organization featherweight champion.
Following Garcia's unanimous victory over former four-time world champion Adrien Broner at the Showtime on Saturday, both fighters will be in the top five of the scales.
Garcia extended her right hand and said, "How are you, champion?
“I wish you good luck.”
Lomachenko will defend his belt with Miguel Marria on Saturday evening at 19 on the ESPN.
When the couple posed for photos, Lomachenko's manager Egis Klimas noted that a possible confrontation between fighters would be a "perfect measurement."
"I'm a former featherweight," Garcia recalled.
"I moved up to [63.5 kg] for Broner, but I'm not that big.
I'm 63.5 kg small.
I want to move below the lightweight.
And he has to move upwards with a light weight [61,2 kg], and then we can fight.
Fans want that.
Let’s give them this.”
Lomachenko agreed.
"The boxing world needs this fight."
Klimas raised the question of whether it should be paid. This is part of his strategy, according to which he wants Lomachenko to debut towards the end of this year in a 61.2 kg class to make it possible for next summer to meet Garcia (37-0, 30 knockouts), who are also interested in fighting the winner of Jorge Linare and Luke Campbell's.
“Watchinging this fight could be paid because fans have wanted this confrontation,” Garcia said, later saying he’s open to fighting on any channel that offers the best financial package.
“We are key players.
No other name can get so much attention.
Whenever they're ready..."
Garcia said he considers Lomachenko to be a "mighty fighter" and he could go and watch his Saturday match.
Lomachenko, however, was modest in praising Garcia, saying that in his opinion, the champion of three divisions from Riverside looked "very slow; I'm sorry, Mikey... you wanted that knockout."
According to Garcia, the goal was to show the impact strength and then box.
Both confirmed that they felt the tension growing between them.
"It's a fight that's waiting to be realized.
No other confrontation generates so much attention and buzz.
"I'm more than willing to work for it," Garcia said.
Lomachenko said: "I've been waiting for the best fighters and Mikey's just that.
It's going to be a good fight."
From which corners of the world are they moving to Estonia?
Last yard, the Police and Border Guard Board (PBGB) extended and issued a record number of more than 13 000 residence permits to third-country nationals who have mostly travelled to Estonia to work, study or have families.
For example, it was decided last year to issue a temporary residence permit to 6702 aliens, but last year the figure was 4928.
Authorities cannot say how many immigrants will eventually remain permanently in Estonia, but such an analysis will be prepared by the Ministry of the Interior.
Ten years ago, nearly 500 Ukrainians moved to Estonia every year, but in recent years this number is beginning to come close to 2000.
The number of applicants for a residence permit increased exponentially during the crisis in Ukraine - in 2014, 1087 people received a temporary residence permit, compared to 675 a year earlier.
According to Statistics Estonia, the number of Ukrainian citizens is increasing the most in Estonia - last year, 850 more Ukrainian citizens arrived in Estonia than left.
"The Ukrainian community has always been large in Estonia, and due to the difficult circumstances in the country, it is natural that jobs and opportunities for learning are sought in a country where the community, as well as the family in front of many," said Kristin Rammus, spokesperson for the Ministry of the Interior.
Last year and last year, the number of Ukrainians who had been granted a temporary residence permit was larger than even the number of citizens of Russia, although in the course of ten years the largest number of fixed-term residence permits have been issued to the latter.
While Ukrainians mostly come to work here, Russian citizens come here for the sake of family migration.
"Taking into account the historical Russian community here, they are also more likely to have the largest family ties with people living in Russia," said Helina Maasing, migration researcher at the Estonian Academy of Security Sciences.
"If you look at where Russian citizens who have been granted an Estonian residence permit have gone to live, Ida-Viru County is just behind Harju County," he added, pointing out that about 40 percent of them have married a citizen of the European Union.
Last year, 1696 Russian citizens received a temporary residence permit, which is also a record number.
At the same time, the number of Russians who wished to extend their residence permit
"The leap is due to the fact that a new residence permit has been applied for to settle permanently in Estonia instead of extending the residence permit," explained Rammus, a spokesperson for the Ministry of the Interior, according to whom the interest of Russian citizens in the Estonian residence permit has remained rather stable for years.
Foreigners who have resided here for at least three years can apply for a new residence permit to settle permanently in Estonia from the previous year.
Its purpose is to facilitate the survival of adapted people.
For example, a long-term resident's residence permit requires, for example, five years.
The extension of an old residence permit has probably also decreased due to a new residence permit.
For example, last year such a residence permit was issued to 146 Ukrainians who have worked here for at least three years.
Now the Ukrainians will be able to change their jobs, and the Estonian state will no longer keep track of whether the wage criterion has been fulfilled.
This residence permit became very popular last year as applicants for gray passport holders (1029), Russians (487), Indians (54) and Nigerians (36).
In total, 1968 people received a residence permit for the purpose of settling in Estonia, including, for example, 23 Americans, 12 Egyptians, 18 Georgians and the same number of Chinese people.
In the first half of this year, the total number of such applicants is already 1269.
Surprisingly in ten years, Nigeria has become a country whose citizens are one of the most active applicants for Estonian residence permits.
While three residence permits were issued to Nigerians in 2007, 200 were issued last year.
People come from Nigeria to study at local universities.
Estonian higher education institutions are also popular, for example, among Georgians, Chinese and Turks.
The number of citizens of India, Bangladesh and Nigeria has risen largely due to student mobility.
There is also a rule where the community has emerged in advance, it will be easier to add others," Rammus added.
Over the course of ten years, two people have also moved to Estonia, for example, from North Korea, the same number from Laose and Madagascar.
Ten people have come here from Cuba, 231 from South Korea, 40 from Indonesia and four from Uruguay.
While about 6000 third-country nationals receive a residence permit per year, this is about 0.46 percent of the total population.
Even if you consider the citizens of the European Union, the migration will remain at about a percentage," Maasing commented that, in fact, the figures are small.
According to Statistics Estonia, a total of 14,822 people migrated to Estonia last year, compared with 13 792.
This means that immigration surpassed emigration for a second year.
Most of those who move out and in are Estonian citizens, but there are more of them among those who leave.
As a result, the migration balance of Estonian citizens was in deficit with 2000 people last year.
Estonians primarily migrate between Finland and Estonia.
Mammu Couture's princess mode charms with sats, embroidery and tulle
However, Maria Tammeoru's box of ideas is history - old paintings and costumes: "I can dream for hours somewhere in the museum and admire old-fashioned sleeves and collars.
If you do something with passion, you'll find out and learn more about it every day.
It's the joy of discovery.
The glamorous world of Mammu Couture may seem alien to some, but the designer doesn't think so.
You decide how much sparkle is in your life.
I've got a friend who's got a whole sauna full of haute couture dresses I've created.
Because it'sn't her fault that she doesn't live in Hollywood and doesn't go on the red carpet every daily, she wants to wear those most beautiful dresses and she wears!
You can live your own life special.
The fashion designer does not recognize the gap between bright events and grey everyday life.
It was precisely this reasoning that made the plan to move high fashion to the countryside in Estonia, between forests and fields, to the people to whom fashion often does not reach.
For example, Mammu's collection was presented in Võru, Jõhvi and Pärnu fashion alley, where a royal defilee with 45 models filled the entire street.
In Tallinn, a pose is often taken in, nodded with dignity, but there are more emotions elsewhere - shout bravo, stand up, want to thank you by your hand.
Much of the audience just came to take a look, but now they're fans of Mammu Couture.
If you exceed people's expectations, they're impressed!
And I like the feeling that I'm on a summer tour with my fashion creations.
In Pärnu, we had a 50-meter-long red rug, a whole street full of beautiful dresses, it seemed that there would be no end!
Maria also does not recognize the fashion practice that creations must be presented by a supermodel, she is happy to bring ordinary women to the stage.
I often invite my own clients to the stage - they have always come with pleasure.
It's such a great experience, you can shine on stage in a beautiful dress!
I'm so sorry I've never seen my show myself, I've always been behind the stage to give things the last touch as a hopeless perfectionist.
My nerves wouldn't let me stand out in the hall.
Maria is the kind of fashion designer who not only wants to play the role of representation, but to be inside everything.
I have my own sewing machine in the studio, most of the collection I do it myself.
I like the whole process where you play with the details, you discover.
I'm never far away from my work, and I'm not afraid of work.
It's not limited to design, including stylistics, logistics, organization, organization - everything has to fit one temperamental woman's day.
These working hours are huge for fanatics.
I don't know when my working day will end, it's just a day full of joy for me!
It's like an incessant chain of happiness: I do something beautiful, a person gets something beautiful, and in turn, compliments.
It has been said many times that there is magic in my dresses - they make me feel like a princess.
Abundant tulle rows that made Maria's talent famous overnight have remained Mammu Couture's signature to this day.
