NATO ships come to Tallinn to replenish supplies
Today, NATO's 1st permanent mine defence group arrived in Tallinn for a scheduled port visit for the weekend to replenish ships' reserves and prepare for upcoming exercises.
During the course of the visit, the ships are preparing to return to the sea and at the same time give crew members the opportunity to take a few days off.
After the visit, NATO vessels will remain in Estonian waters to carry out a series of exercises and be ready for future operations for the search for historical explosive devices.
"The participation of Estonians in the NATO unit and the cooperation training carried out by them in local waters illustrates our integration with allied forces," said Commander-in-Chief of the Operations and Planning Division Arto Reinmaa, who is the commander of the Naval Fleet.
As of 29 June, Lieutenant Commander Gvido Laudups, a Latvian navyman, will lead NATO's 1st permanent anti-mine defence group, who took over the tasks of a group leader from Estonian naval officer Lieutenant Commander Johan-Elias Seljamaa at a ceremony in Riga.
The group's flagship is the Latvian navy support vessel LVNS Virsaitis, on which the group's command staff also operates.
In this semester, Sakala, an Estonian naval minehunter, will also belong to the international unit.
As one of the four groups of ships under the command of the NATO Naval Command, NATO's 1st permanent mine control group is a readiness unit belonging to its primary rapid responders, whom the alliance can send to various parts of the world within five days.
From 4 to 13 August, the ancient ruins of Pirita Convent will once again become a place where the Birgitta Festival, which was founded 13 years ago by maestro Eri Klas, will be held.
According to Risto Joost (36), the artistic director of the Tallinn Philharmonic Harmony, this year's Birgitta Festival will be beautiful, powerful and unprecedented.
Risto Joost: "Traviata" and "Tosca", ballet productions from "Bolero" to the amusing "Karlsson", which belongs to the classics of the opera world, will reach the audience, and neither will self-production, which this time is the production of Joseph Haydn's wonderful oratorio "Seasons" by the same international creative team, who in 2015 brought Haydn to the stage.
Birgitta Festival is much more than just concerts and theatre productions.
It's bigger than any musical theatre in its usual form.
It's a festival whose enchantement takes over and will never let go again.
For the experience that the listener receives in this sublime and mysterious environment makes human souls ring and touches human perceptions in a way that cannot be described.
Risto, for the first year you are the artistic director of the Birgitta Festival.
Weren't maestro Eri Klas's shoes a little big?
Of course, but since I had a very personal relationship with Eri Klas, it's been very helpful.
He guided me throughout my student career and also supported me at the beginning of my conducting career.
Maybe that's why it's easier for me to continue his work.
Eri Klas has injected so much kindness into the Birgitta Festival team and, of course, all of this is transferred to me.
It was great to join a team that knows exactly how things are going.
It was the great heart of Eri Klas that gave me the strength to move forward with the Birgitta Festival at the Tallinn Philharmonic Society.
And here I am, and I'll make it!
What could excite the audience at this year's festival, what do you offer special?
We have artists from England, Latvia, Lithuania and, as a surprise, the first ballet written for Astrid Lindgren's story will be presented.
That's "Karlsson flies."
And one curious nuance is that this ballet was written by the Latvian composer Juris Karlsons, who himself expressed his desire to write this work.
Apparently, he's had Karlsson's character in his soul since he was a child.
Risto, how do you manage all this?
Because of my supportive partner.
This kind of work needs a support system and a lot of love, and luckily I have a lot of it.
And I've noticed that my personal emotions don't depend so much on other people as if on me, that is, how I meet each new day.
And this kind of conscious maintenance of tone, maintaining positivity and actually smilinging yourself in the mirror in the mornings, significantly increased my happiness percentage.
Especially now this week, when there are the most important rehearsals, I've been practicing it especially eagerly and it works.
Smilinging activates the brain and raises tone.
It's not just a buzzword.
I don't think people yet know half of the things about their bodies that the universe offers them, but everyone is slowly discovering it.
Immediately, it'll be an important moment.
Do you have a ritual before the conductor's stand?
It is, clothes should be replaced with more comfortable ones.
It's that little moment for myself.
But in general, yes, I don't have the opportunity to rest or take a little nap before a big performance.
In fact, I even like to actively interact with people, even before stepping on the conductor's office.
But come to the Birgitta Festival, here's something to discover for a not very big friend of operas and ballets!
Watch a spirited video with Risto Joost!
New results of Swedish asylum seeker age assessment tests published
In Sweden, a test was carried out among asylum seekers, during which their age was judged medically with a novel approach. The most recent results of the test show that more than 80 percent of those tested were judged older than 18, but not everyone believes in the accuracy of the results.
The Swedish National Forensic Medicine Institute (Rättsmedicinalverket) has launched tests earlier this year.
The tests were created for a more accurate age assessment in the course of applying for asylum after the Swedish Migration Service (Migrationsverket) was criticized for failing to correctly determine the age of several allegedly juvenile refugees.
To date, Migrationsverket has sent 6,880 cases for testing, and the Forensic Medicine Institute has issued a total of 2481 test results between mid-March and 31 July 2017.
Eighty percent (2002) were rated 18 or older, and in another 25 cases, the Forensic Medicine Institute estimated that the "may be 18 years of age or older".
In 432 cases, it was concluded that a person is "probably younger than 18".
Tests are used only if a person's age is in question.
According to Johan Göransson, who works at the Forensic Medicine Institute, the results are consistent compared to those of previous months.
"We don't see a big difference compared to the statistics of previous months, the numbers are quite consistent," he told The Local.
The fact that a large number of cases were rated older than 18 does not necessarily mean that asylum seekers have lied about their ages.
The institute issued 581 test results in May and said that in 442 cases "there are indications that the person under investigation is 18 years of age or older."
But when newspaper Svenska Dagbladet examined the results of these 581 tests, it turned out that in 243 cases a person had directly claimed to turn 18 this year.
This method of age assessment, in the course of which X-rays of wisdom teeth and MRI from the knee joints are carried out, after which they are allowed to be analyzed by dentists and radiologists, has been criticized in some circles.
One of the skeptics is endocrinologist Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute, whose duties include assessing the biological maturity of people to find out whether they went through puberty sooner or later than usual.
"I've had reason to think about it, and I don't think that the current method of measurement should be used.
Determining biological age in this way is very uncertain.
If we can't say for sure, then we shouldn't pretend we can," he told Metro.
But Johan Göransson from the Institute of Forensic Medicine defended the accuracy of the tests: "In general, it can be said that there is a low risk of an imprecisely dividing a child into adulthood.
At about 10%, there is the greatest risk if a person is a little less than 18."
9 Signs You Can Forgive a Deceived Lover.
Your boyfriend betrayed you and spent the night with someone else.
You're in pain because you've got so many wonderful times and memories together.
You'd like to forgive him, but isn't it the right time...
Your Tango lists nine signs that your loved one regrets what he does and could forgive him.
Your boyfriend seems to understand the seriousness of the situation and goes to a lot of trouble.
Your boyfriend's dealing with a problem that took him to bed with his other partner.
Your sweetheart won't be defensive anymore.
He sincerely regrets and takes responsibility for his actions.
He won't try to accuse you of his behavior.
He agrees that he has made a serious mistake and sincerely tries to remedy the situation.
He's attentive and he's trying to communicate pleasantly with you.
At the same time, he'll put pressure on you in a hurry to get over it.
They don't deny what's happened, and they don't try to make it unnoticed.
You and your loved one have an understanding of why infidelity arose towards you.
You will talk about it and how to avoid it in the future.
You will learn to communicate better and assess each other's needs.
Your sweetheart's attitudes and actions have helped you to develop compassion for your mistake.
The dedication and behavior of your loved one has created the basis that your intimacy begins to recover.
As a couple, you have made progress to believe in your unity and relationship again.
