The governor of West Virgina announced a party changeover
West Virginia's Democrat governor, Jim Justice, announced at a gathering with President Trump on Thursday that he was switching the party.
"Like it or not, Democrats were walking away from me," Governor Justice said.
"Today I tell you, having prayed a lot and thought... today I will tell you, the Western Virginians, that I will no longer be able to help you as a democratic governor."
Cheerful cheers were released at the gathering.
"So I'm going to be a Republican tomorrow," Justice added.
"As a coach, I'll tell you it's time to play another game."
Trump had previously pledged a major announcement at a gathering in West Virginia.
Trump scored 42 points in West Virginia, and Justice didn't support Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton.
According to Forbes, Justice, the state's only billionaire, was elected in 2016.
Forbes notes that he owns coal mines in five states, and he inherited the coal business from his father.
His value is approximately 1.59 billion dollars.
At the same time, the Republican National Committee (RNC) announced on Thursday evening that "the change of Governor Justice's party is yet another blow to a democratic party that would rather prevent the country from getting better again than working for it."
"Just 15 governorships and control of the lowest number of states in history -- Governor Justice's announcement is simply the most recented rejection of a party that's end-to-end and unable to find a positive and unifying message," RNC chairwoman Ronna McDaniel told CBS News in a statement.
