Coe praised the Russians' honest apology for the doping scandal
Athletics international leader Sebastian Coe said he praised "Russia's Thursday Honest Apology" for the doping scandal, but reiterated that now is not the time to allow the country back into the sport.
Dmitry Shylakhtin, Russia's athletics champion, said at an IAAF congress on the eve of the World Athletics Championships that the ban imposed on his country is correct and that he has a firm goal to combat doping.
He apologized to "all track and field athletes who have been snatched off gold and silver medals in competitions."
Despite his comments, the IAAF Congress voted in favour of maintaining a ban on competition imposed in November 2015 after a report by the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) found evidence of state-sponsored doping in Russia.
"In my opinion, this was a very honest answer today, a very honest presentation," IAAF President Coe told reporters.
"The entire council and the entire congress were pleased that the Russian Federation itself admitted that it had been quite difficult times and that they would do everything possible to reorganize the federation."
According to Coe, he was also pleased that Russia accepted the criteria for reinstating itself.
"I believe it was a very constructive day today and progress is taking place, but Congress supports the working group's proposal that now is not the right time for Russia's withdrawal," Coe said.
"We have to do everything possible in the next few months to normalize the situation.
We have always proceeded from the principle that we want to separate clean athletes from the violated system," he added.
Nineteen Russian athletic athletes who meet the IAAF's strict criteria will compete as neutral athletes at the World Athletics Championships starting in London on Friday.
Rune Andersen, leader of the IAAF's team, said on Monday that Russia does not yet meet a number of reinstatement criteria.
Following a presentation by the IAAF's supervisory board, he told reporters in London that doping control was still inadequate, and coaches who were banned were still operating freely.
The electric car revolution is significantly changing the demand for metals.
Who are winners and losers?
Not only is the electric car revolution significantly changing everything from the fuel market to the infrastructure, it also creates new winners and losers in the metal market.
While mining giants like Glencore can say they win from one place and lose from the other, smaller mining companies won't help.
Glencore currently earns from coal and oil, but from now on in the era of electric cars, the demand for cobalt, lithium, copper, aluminum and nickel will increase.
Decisions from the UK, French and Norwegian governments have raised tributes to the spread of electric cars this year, promising to ban the sale of new cars using fossil fuels in the coming decades.
That's why Volvo has already known that they are planning to abandon an internal combustion engine, and Tesla is launching a cheaper Model 3 electric car.
According to Bloomberg New Energy Finance, more electric cars will be solded over two decades than cars using petroleum-produced fuels.
"For some metals, it will change everything," said Simona Gambranini, a commodity analyst at Capital Economics.
We've already seen a big impact on metals such as cobalt and lithium, whose demand has been growing for years.
There are about three times more copper in electric cars than in a conventional car, Glencore reported.
Even more copper is needed at charging stations, which is why Exane BNP Paribas estimates that it will increase copper demand by about five percent by 2025.
An increase in the demand for batteries for electric cars means additional demand for lithium, cobalt, graphite and manganese.
Glencore will profit from the growing electric car trend from copper price rises and being the world's largest cobalt producer, the Jefferies Group predicted.
According to this brokeraging company, other winners from the triumph of electric cars are Freeport-McMoRan and First Quantum Minerals.
The market is already reacting to a changing trend.
This year's 70 percent increase in cobalt on the London Metal Exchange.
Last year's price increase was 37 percent.
Corpse has also risen by 14 percent this year, mainly due to economic growth.
Glencore's stock rose 20 percent in London, having outperformed rivals Rio Tinto Group, BHP Billiton and Anglo America.
On the losing side, there are lead companies like Recyclex and Campine, which must adapt to a new era.
The main end-use of lead is the power-supplied batteries for petrol and diesel car starters.
Electric cars use lithium-ion batteries.
"It's a serious threat to lead demand unless it's found to have another purpose that could overcome a drop in demand," said Michael Widmer, Head of Metal Analysis at Bank of America Merrill Lynch.
In the form of lead price movement, there is still no effect of the unfolding trend.
Lead has appreciated by 17 percent this year, making it the best metal among major industrial metals in London.
Investors will see problems for the metal in the distant future.
"I'm not sure it's so bad for lead, because cheap oil keeps conventional cars competitive," said Herwig Schmidt, head of sales at metal brokerage Triland Metalts.
If the demand for lead drops, it happens slowly.
This may happen in about ten years.
In the meantime, however, stricter emission norms will mean a growing demand for hybrid cars, which will depend on lead-based batteries that have developed in frequent engine start-ups, is estimated by the International Lead and Zinc Research Organisation.
Light metals such as aluminum replace steel, as a lighter car can travel further with less electricity.
This explains an additional demand of 1.6 million tonnes of aluminium already until 2013-2016.
It accounts for 2.7 percent of the world's global aluminium production.
It's likely this trend will worsen, Widmer said.
Aluminium has appreciated by 13% this year as increasing demand from car manufacturers has led to a market deficit.
Steel producers don't sleep.
AK Steel Holding is working with General Motors to use nanotechnology to produce suctaneous car bodies.
ArcelorMittal and Tata Steel Europe are also trying to develop a lighter alloy of steel to resist aluminum.
"Huge steel should be a response to this development," said Sylvain Brunet, an analyst at Exane BNP Paribas.
There will be some progress in Europe.
The end of the oil age can also bring suffering to the platinum.
Last year, about half of this precious metal turned into catalysts for diesel-powered cars, according to metal refiner Johnson Matthey.
"Many commodities currently in high demand, such as petroleum and platinum, may not have high demand in the future," said Bernard Dahdah, commodity analyst at Natixis.
This does not mean that raw materials as a whole will become less important, but their importance will change within 15 years.
Platinum manufacturers believe in continued demand for diesel hybrid cars.
Marc Grynberg, head of Uncore, said more hybrid cars will continue to be soldied than all-electric cars in 2025.
Platinum manufacturers’ high hopes of 10 years have come from hydrogen-based cars developed by Toyota Motor and Hyundai Motos, which depend on platinum to produce electricity from hydrogen.
Until now, however, this technology has not justified itself commercially.
"Diesel seems to be a clear loser on the way to electric cars," said Brunet from Exane BNP Paribas.
German automakers suffered a major setback in July after a Stuttgart court banned the use of diesel in the home town of Mercedes-Benz and Porsche.
How football's financial rules affect Neymar's transition to Paris
The rules aimed at preventing unbridled spending of European football clubs do not prevent Neymar's world-record transfer from the Spanish football club to Barcelona's French club Paris-Saint Germain.
Any consequences from UEFA, the governing body that rules the European game, which has the power to prevent teams from taking part in the prestigious Champions League tournament, Paris Saint-Germain is still waiting.
UEFA's 2011 Fair Play Financial Rules (FFPs) means that PSG will have to prove at some time that Neymar's transition was financed without huge losses.
Barcelona were paid 222 million euros (262 million dollars) just to buy the Brazilian from his contract, and Neymar's salary will reach tens of millions of dollars a year.
Here are some financial aspects of Neymar's transfer to the French club.
PSG will undoubtedly be able to spend such money to recruit Neymar and add it to its starful line-ups, given that the team will be funded by the family of Qatar's rich energy.
Clubs are allowed to spend a lot to recruit players, but they have to balance it with legal sources of income that allow them to come up with their own in the football business.
The FFP was founded by Michel Platini after he was elected UEFA president in 2007.
This former French player believed that clubs who fall into big debts in search of success are actually dealing with cheating and risk ruining if banks or owners should give up support.
Since 2011, the FFP has been focusing on tracking their losses instead of club debt.
This change means that the business model that Roman Abramovich first used in Chelsea in 2003 – buying a club with the potential and then spending heavily in order to improve the team and create a global brand – is more difficult to implement.
During the current three-year evaluation period of the FFP, which lasts until 2018, clubs can suffer losses of up to 30 million euros.
And even though Platini was disgraced from the presidency in 2015, his UEFA successor Alexander Ceferin is still committed to preventing excessive spending.
"The FFP is certainly not dead, and we will undoubtedly continue to strengthen these rules in the future," Andrea Traverso told The Associated Press last week, who is an FFP supervisor at UEFA.
Although PSG paid Neymar's overburse clause as a total payout, they will be able to split this amount for accounting purposes throughout the duration of the contract.
Neymar entered into a five-year contract, which is why it is possible to indicate the transfer fee over the next five years as an amount of 44.4 million euros per year.
On the occasion of the first wave of FFP Sanctions in 2014, PSG, along with Abu Dhabi-based Manchester City, received the most severe penalties.
PSG was fined 60 million euros (currently 82 million dollars), which was later reduced to 20 million euros, and the club was required to siege the Champions League team in the 2014-2015 season for 21 players instead of the usual 25.
UEFA is looking for clubs that can try to outsmart over regulatory financial institutions by blowing up their earnings through companies affiliated with club owners.
The Qatari company may sponsor PSG, but if the value of the sponsorship is considered to exceed standard market rates, UEFA would not allow it to be accounted for as an official income.
This is what happened in 2014, when UEFA decided that the value of support from the Qatar Tourism Authority PSG had been blown up.
It has been said that a certain part of Neymar's PSG service could come from Qatar through payments from the organizing committee of the 2022 World Cup.
However, PSG and Katar 2022 deny that Neymar could become a World Cup envoy, and argue that UEFA regulators can easily reveal such a manoeuvre.
Penalties previously imposed on PSG mean that UEFA is even more vigilant with regard to any efforts to ignore the rules.
People familiar with the situation said Thursday that UEFA has not proactively discussed Neymar's transition with PSG.
They spoke to The Associated Press on the condition that their anonymity is ensured, as they are not allowed to discuss the PSG situation.
The most recently published reports show that PSG earned more than 10 million euros in profit at around 542,4 million euros in the 2015-2016 season.
The data for the last financial year have not yet been published.
PSG expects Neymar's arrival to have a positive effect on sponsorship transactions, shopping and shirt sales.
Due to the conclusion of the contract, the club expects an increase of at least 30 percent in turnover.
The Spanish league, clearly upset about the departure of one of the biggest football stars, tried to stop Neymar's transfer, arguining that PSG was violating the FFP.
"This is a clear case of 'monetary doping' with the club and the state," La Liga president Javier Tebas told Spanish newspaper AS, pointing out that PSG belonged mainly to Qatar.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger, whose club belongs to an American sports magnate, shares La Liga's attitude that the country is funding the club.
"This is a consequence of property rights and it has completely changed the entire football scene over the last 15 years," Wenger said on Thursday.
"If the club belongs to the state, anything is possible.
It is very difficult to respect the financial rules of fair play, since the country may have other ways or other interests so that such a large player would represent the country."
The Norwegian low-cost airline Norwegian has a nice tradition of putting pictures of those who have pushed the boundaries in their field and been an inspiration to others, writes trip.ee
