Among the selected individuals are Benjamin Franklin, Greta Garbo (pictured) and Miguel de Cervantes.
In the near future, however, a portrait of Queen's legendary singer Freddie Mercury will decorate a plane.
50 years ago today: hurricane-sized storm in Estonia, which tore roofs and windows from houses.
The hurricane, called the storm of the century, raged on 6 and 7 August, caused major damage primarily to Lääne County, Harju County and Rapla County.
In North-Western Estonia, during those days, the wind speed was more than 30 m/s.
My old man ran from a curved tree to the other in the garden and tried with his hands to support them.
Everything was blind and crackled like the end of the world.
Roofs already flew from houses in the distance, old lady Linda Kurova from Haapsalu recalled in 2002 in an interview with Eesti Loodus magazine.
Linda's husband saw how the garden he planted collapsed.
But what do you support the trees with your hands, where are you going to face a storm like this?
The sea came under the window, water was splashering through the windows," said Mrs. Linda, who lived on Kalda Street in Haapsalu at the time.
The fact that the roads were full of trees and the roof of the railway station's platform had flown 20-30 meters away, Linda did not saw the likes of most Haapsalu people until the next morning.
According to the locals, there were trees behind Paralepa, some completely lying down, some in half.
Everything looked very frustrating, the storm went through beautiful old trees with a ragging and crackling like a war.
Lääne-Viru County did not suffer particularly severely compared to Western Estonia in the August 1967 storm, but the damage was also great there, Virumaa Teataja wrote in 2007.
Haapsalu Castle Park and Padise Park were wiped from the face of the country, but the parks of our area were also severely damaged.
In Tallinn, the wind speed was up to 35 meters per second, or about 130 kilometers per hour.
On the evening of 6 August, a little girl had shouted: "Mom, mother!
Chickens are in the air and can't get down anymore!", wrote Virumaa Teataja.
Põlli Manor building, located in Viljandi County, was so destroyed during the storm that it was later decided to demolish it.
The constructions of Türi ski jumping hill, which were no longer restored later, did not withstand the August storm.
On August 12, 1967, the Red Star newspaper wrote that cleaning up towns and settlements from fallen trees and their branches is progressing slowly, and residents could get involved in the clean-ups.
Grain fields were also severely damaged in Storm, and the newspaper reported that "party early society must be inspirers and guides in both reorganization and grain harvesting."
Hurricanes can be talked about if the average wind speed exceeds 32.7 m/s.
The outbreak wind record in Estonia will last from November 2, 1969, when Ruhnu measured the wind speed of 48 m/s.
On Vaindloo Island, a measuring instrument was broken, so it was not possible to accurately record the speed of the strong wind measured there.
The secret of clean beaches: night cleaning and poor beach weather
Colder summer weather also means a smaller cleaning effort in Tallinn's bathing areas.
"If there's weather, there's people on the beach, and if there aren't, there aren't people," admits the beach cleaner.
Seagulls are screaming on the beach of Stroom in Northern Tallinn.
They are offered melodic competition by a low-rev tractor that ploughs down stripes on the slate.
In fact, the latter sifts through the sand at a depth of seven centimetres, picking out marine debris and people's abandoned rubbish: bottle caps, cigarette cigarette butts, and more.
People still come to the beach, regardless of whether the water is warm or not.
If there is beautiful weather, then there are people on the beach, and if there are none, there are no people," says Aleksandr Usov, head of the clean-up works of the private limited company Haabersti Haldus.
It's 6.30 a.m., and there's a bitter clean-up on the beach, because at eight o'clock the place has to be in order for people.
Usov's on duty from four o'clock.
In addition to Stroom, he also keeps an eye on Pirita, Lake Harku, Kakumäe and Paljasaare Pikakari beach.
Garbage has been reduced.
Years ago, there was much more.
People now know how to appreciate what is being done for them," says Usov, who has been cleaning the beaches for 15 years.
Before, when something new was erected, it was broken the next day.
They’re keeping what’s on the beach.
The price of a clean beach
One of the biggest works on Estonian beaches is the cleaning-up of sea garbage or plough washed ashore.
For example, 400 000 tons have to be transported during the beach season on Pirita beach.
"It's an incredible quantity," Usov notes.
The second large category consists of household waste, that is, everything that people leave behind.
The one-and-a-half-kilometer Stroomi beach strip has eight large garbage containers that need to be emptied three times a week.
There's so much garbage.
However, colder summer weather has meant that there is a fifth less garbage than it would otherwise.
The third species consists of abandoned things.
Most of these are children's lives.
As this year's prey, Usov has shoveled a dozen model cars out of the sand.
They are waiting for the owners on the wooden road leading to the beach.
"Usually, more of them will come out, but this is a normal prey". Usov already knows that soon he will find them again in the sand.
The children come to the beach and take toys to drown them in the sand again.
Garbage is scattered
Erika Scholler, representative of Haabersti administrative services, admits that the screening of the tractor is disturbed by the locals.
Noise, which lasts from night to morning, interferes with the sleep of people living in the neighborhood.
However, this work is impossible during the day.
The beach has to be siftered twice a month.
"If we don't do this, the district will fine us," Scholler notes.
However, similarly to Pirita, Pikakari and Pärnu, the goal is to get Stroomi beach under the international blue flag quality label.
"Compared to Europe, there is much more work in Estonia to ensure that the beaches are maintained," says Scholler.
We have more ploughs and rocks, and it's harder to sieve sand.
However, there is less garbage on our beaches than anywhere else in Europe.
Hearing that the beacfare is talking about rubbish, Martin, who lives near Stroomi beach, enters a discussion group of journalists and cleaning workers, highlighting the current bottlenecks on the beach.
There's a disgusting picture everywhere in the morning.
There's a problem with birds and bottle pickers who've pulled the garbage bags upside down," Martin reports.
Although it is difficult to assume that people will be able to change their current behavioural habits, Martin hopes that the city government will do something with trash cans and, for example, put lids on them to prevent at least the birds from exploring there.
In general, however, Martin is satisfied with the development of Stroom Beach.
You can see the city takes care of it.
With children's squares, illuminated and freshly asphalted roads, it has become more pleasant.
Once upon a time, it was only possible to ride a roller skate on one road," Martin notes.
Treasure hunter's findings in Stroom: coins, only coins
40-year-old Dimitri has come to take advantage of the morning low season, combing the coastal side with a metal detector as a treasure hunter.
He doesn't think he'll find anything precious today or in the future.
Sea gods are not generous enough for this.
"I've dealt with it for a year and haven't found anything valuable yet," Dmitri says.
As a hobby treasure hunter, Dmitri's treasure bag has some euro coins, bottle caps, and also dangerous nails that will later throw in the trash.
There are usually coins.
"Gold, rings and there's usually no such thing," admits the treasure hunter.
The Bank of England keeps interest rates at 0.25%
The Bank of England is expected to report that interest rates will remain at the same level as powerless economic growth and easing inflation have softened speculation about the rise.
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain sluggish, as households' squeezing incomes have put interest rates on hold.
The Bank's Monetary Policy (MPC's) policy makers voted in favour of keeping 6:2 at 0.25%, and fewer members are demanding an increase this month as slow economic growth has weakened support for growth.
In its quarterly inflation report, the bank cut its growth forecast for 2017 to 1.7% and its 1.6% in 2018, and warned that the pressure on household incomes would continue, as inflation is still expected to rise close to 3% in the autumn.
At the same time, the bank also signaled that an increase in rates over the next few years is necessary to curb inflation caused by Brexit, and financial markets may need a higher-than-expected increase in borrowing costs.
Members also voted a year ago in favour of the partial readmission of the mammoth package that boosted the economy as a result of Brexit.
The bank will end a fixed-term financing scheme in February, which will provide cheap funding to banks, although it said it now expects a 15-billion-pound supply of 115 billion pounds.
In the minutes of the decision on the rate, the bank says: "Gross domestic production (GDP) will remain powerless in the near future, as the difficult situation of actual household earnings will continue to have an impact on consume."
As regards rates, it was reiterated that in order to mitigate inflatment, it is necessary to "slightly tighten monetary policy" to the extent of "somewhat higher" than is expected in the markets.
The stock market is currently predicting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and a second increase in 2020.
However, the bank emphasized that all jumps are gradual and limited.
The bank's reduced growth forecasts for this year and the following year are 1.9% and 1.7% comparable to May's forecasts.
The forecast for growth of 1.8 per cent in 2018 was maintained.
After this news, the pound fell against the dollar and the euro.
The pound fell by 0.5% to 1.31 US dollars and 0.4% to 1.11 euros.
The decision not to make changes is due to the recent disappointing growth figures, which have dampened rising hopes due to an increase ▖ GDP rose from 0.2% in the previous three months at 0.3% in the second quarter.
The bank says that growth is likely to remain at 0.3% in the third quarter, even though it will start to gain momentum at the end of the year.
The bank's most recent inflation report gave little reason to those suffering from rising inflation and wrestled wages, saying that the situation is getting a little worse before it improves.
It was added that monetary policy cannot prevent a hit in the next few years, but a significant recovery in wages is expected towards the end of the three-year forecast.
Due to the weak pound, the economy is also stimulated by the increasing demand for British production, which partially compensates consumers for lower costs.
The decision comes a year after the rates were moved 0.20% last August after a shocking EU referendum that caused a sudden weakening of the pound.
According to the bank, it will probably take four years for prices and the economy to fully breathe life.
Prior to the bank's latest decision, there had been increasing demand for raising the rate, as inflation had increased the pressure on heavily households; in July, three policymakers demanded an increase of 0.5%.
Inflation fell to 2.6% last month, compared to June 2.9%, although according to the bank, this was as expected and will rise again in the coming months.
The bank said that excessive growth compared to the 2 percent target was due to a completely weak pound.
Andrew Montlake, head of Coreco's Mortgage Brokers, said: "Given a recent drop in exchange rates, the mortgage market seems to be becoming increasingly competitive again as lenders crave work and hope to end the year in a positive way.
The stock exchange seems particularly advantageous for those whose current transactions are coming to an end, as refinancing, often without a service fee and at a low rate, has become increasingly popular."
The NHS is about to prescribe HIV drugs that have sparked controversy among 10 000 people
Since September, patients have had access to "transformative" HIV preventive treatment after a battle in the Supreme Court over whether the NHS should finance the drug.
NHS England announced that during the initial three-year clinical test, national healthcare is now making an estimated 10,000 people available pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP), a tablet administered before intercourse, which has shown a reduction in the risk of infection by approximately 86% for high-risk people.
The NHS agreed to fund a clinical trial after the Court of Appeal backed the Supreme Court's last year's ruling that the NHS would be able to pay for PrEP, despite the agency's own allegations that the medicine should be financed by local authorities instead.
Simon Stevens, chief executive of NHS England, greeted it as a "new extensive intervention" of a move unveiled on Thursday, while the National AIDS Fund (NAT) described it as a "reversible moment."
Sexual health clinics in London, Brighton, Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield are among the first to start registeringing people. There will be even more clinics available in October, before the program is deployed anywhere in England by April 2018.
Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis (PrEP) is a course of HIV drugs that are taken before intercourse to reduce the risk of contracting HIV.
The results of clinical trials show that PrEP significantly reduces the transmission of HIV among people at risk of infection.
Taking prEP before intercourse means that there are a sufficient number of certain drugs in the human body that prevent infection with HIV.
Such drugs can be taken every day or at the latest two hours before intercourse.
Appropriate candidates for treatment include gay or bisexual men, transgender people and people whose partners have been infected with HIV and do not receive treatment.
Clinicians identify suitable people who are willing to participate in the clinical trial, including men, women, transgender people and those whose HIV partner is not receiving antiretroviral therapy.
People who live in England and have chosen a family doctor there can also register their wish to participate in a local sexual health clinic participating in the program.
The program costs 10 million pounds, and NHS England signed an agreement to get the medicines this week after an international procurement procedure.
The purpose of the clinical trial is to assess the true potential of PrEP by gathering clinical evidence of for whom the drug is intended and how it is consumed and applied in the big picture.
Stevens said: "This new large-scale intervention should complement and amplify a wide-ranging and increasingly successful fight against HIV transmission.
It's just another milestone for more than three decades of fighting one of humanity's biggest health problems."
Deborah Gold, CEO of NAT, said: "This is a pivotal moment in the fight against HIV.
PrEP, if it is correctly targeted to those who need it and belong to the risk group, offers the opportunity to reverse the HIV epidemic in England.
Since September, people at high risk of HIV in England have access to a powerful new tool that is individually manageable and does not depend on negotiations with a partner through this NHS-fundeded clinical trial.
We warmly welcome this announcement.”
Ian Green, CEO of the Terrence Higgins Foundation, said: "It is now essential to ensure that a clinical trial across the country is launched urgently and that no one at-risk of HIV stays aside.
Now that PrEP's clinical trial drug has been obtained, we are on our way to protecting more than 10,000 people who are at risk of contracting HIV.
Sharon Hodgson said: "The start of a clinical trial in PrEP is very welcome and severely overdue after months of delays and dragging the government's feet.
Evidence shows how big changes this drug can bring about the prevention of HIV and the cessation of the spread of this life-changing infection.
This clinical test takes us one step closer to a full understanding of the benefits of PrEP.
Now it is important that a clinical trial across the country is introduced as soon as possible in order to protect people who are exposed to HIV and take us one step closer to stopping the spread of HIV in society."
The price of electricity increased by 12% in Estonia, while in Latvia and Lithuania it fell.
In July, the next-day difference in the exchange price of electricity decreased to 1.94 euros between the price areas of Estonia and Latvia and Lithuania, compared to 7.71 euros in June this year, Elering said.
The main reason for the price difference is Lithuanian-Swedish connection failures.
In Estonia, the average exchange price of electricity last month was 34.33 euros per megawatt-hour, and the price rose by 12% compared to June.
In Latvia and Lithuania, the price fell by five percent to 36.27 euros per megawatt-hour.
In Finland, the price was 34.17 euros and the system price of the Nord Pool power exchange was 26.37 euros per megawatt-hour.
Prices increased by 12 and seven percent, respectively.
Between Estonia and Finland, 75 percent of last month's hours moved from Estonia to Finland and 16 per cent from Estonia to Finland.
There were no next-day transactions between the two countries in the remaining nine percent of hours.
Between Estonia and Latvia, electricity moved from Estonia to Latvia for 99 percent of the time, and 73% of the hours had total transmission capacity in use.
According to transactions on the Nasdaq OMX market held on the last trading day of July, the exchange price of electricity in August can be approximately 34 euros per megawatt-hour.
The price of carbon dioxide, which affects the production price of electricity, increased last month and transactions took place at a price of 5.04-5.47 euros per tonne.
Living high - half-floor and clever design tricks have turned this small Earls Court apartment into a spacious home
Joanne Leigh, a former banker who is a passionate real estate innovator, switched his large Knightsbridge home to Earls Court's apartment in 2014.
It is located on the ground floor of Queen Anne Terrace, and the fact that Joanne has not packed her bags and reselled the apartment is a tribute to the new design.
"Before that, I lived on a much larger ground in Knightsbride with my former partner," Joanne said.
"I was looking for something that would be equivalent for the price I offered.
I was shown this Earls Court apartment and it had the same high ceilings.
I fell in love with it and made an offer the same day.”
Since the first visit, this 93-square-meter apartment has undergone a complete renovation.
In the beginning, it was an apartment with a single bedroom and a complex layout - to reach the bedroom, you had to walk through the kitchen. Joanne wanted to add storage space and a half-floor to take advantage of high ceilings.
"I wanted high ceilings, large windows, a lot of light.
That was my requirements," he said.
He hired Duck & Shed, an architecture and design studio based in Erfan Azad Notting Hill, to take advantage of 3.8 m high ceilings and create a home that wouldn't just be a development site, but a place where Joanne could live.
The ceiling was not high enough to add two rooms on top of each other, so it was an architectural challenge to create a half-floor where you could stand upright.
Erfan had to approach it creatively.
"Metal construction is quite complex," he explains.
"We had to rearrange the premises so that they would be located on top of each other without having to bend down.
It was not just the construction of the platform, but we used the steel structure so that the height of both the upper and lower spaces would be correct."
This arrangement allowed Erfan to create two full-height rooms on top of each other in the room, along with plenty of dramatic elements.
From the half-floor there is a view of the glamorous living room at the bottom from the old-fashioned Retrouvius window.
Gold furniture with a mirrored surface, silvery details and a retreated Serge Mouille luminaire accentuate the style of the room.
The use of mirrors plays a role in adding theatricality, spaciousness and brightness.
Mirrors have been added at both ends of the short corridor to make it look taller.
In the kitchen, the back wall with a mirror gives the impression of a window that opens into another room.
At the entrance, the mirror cabinet provides a storage space for all Joanne cutlery and glassware.
The possibility to close the part of the kitchen when it is not used adds charm to the living room, creating an idea of a glamorous space.
Folding kitchens are nothing new.
However, there is a unique fact in this kitchen that it is a retractable door of the garage type.
"There was a bit of design effort in this," Erfan explains with steel reinforcement from veneered MDF door.
"It's a counterweight, which's why it glides very easily if you push it up."
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage, Joanne now has 16 extra square meters, an additional bedroom and a better apartment plan.
"At the end of the day, it's what you're selling -- or the one that adds value to the property," says Erfan.
"A real estate agent walks into an outdated form and performs calculations, based on the area of the floor."
Here they have been able to enlarge the floor area using high-quality materials that add luxuriousness to the room.
"Materials are the ones that you touch and thus help create an emotional connection with your home," says Erfan.
"You won't fall in love with the electrical cabling in the wall.
It's important, but it doesn't make your heart ring."
It shows that a good design and an enlarged area will help to make a fairly ordinary apartment something amazing.
In fact, they may even make you want not to leave, as happened with Joanne.
Dane Jensen is a bounty hunter working in a Chicago skyscraper who competes with a colleague for a promotion.
It counts other than the amount of money earned, next to which any ethics is forgotten.
A man sees his wife and three children only early in the morning and late in the evening.
In the first half of the film, I find myself thinking that it's a version of "House of Card" in the business world - even Kevin Spacey and Gerard Butler's manner of speaking is similar.
As the film proceeds, however, there will be less of the frame-like element, and the moralizing intent of the filmmakers is starting to stand out more and more.
A life-changing experience for the protagonist becomes the son's illness with cancer.
Now you have to make a choice between work and family life.
A son dreaming of an architect makes his father think about really important things such as faith, kindness, love during his walks to the great buildings of Chicago, even the disaster of the Twin Towers will not be left untouched.
After the third scene, in which the son opens his father's eyes, there is a feeling that the filmmakers have proceeded from the goal of the audience to definitely cry, because even the viewer with the darkest heart is unable to hold back the tears in front of the suffering of a cute cancerous boy.
When the son falls into a coma, and his situation seems almost hopeless, the father finally understands the immorality of his work and commits a good deed for an old man looking for work.
Now I bet with myself in my mind - if the film has a happy self, I will reward my insight after the movie with coffee, but if I am mistaken, then the screenwriter can be praised for at least one surprise.
For a moment, there is a suspicion that I will miss out on coffee - when her husband calls Dane from the hospital and the man slowly rushes down the corridor towards the ward.
The son, however, has miraculously healed, and although his father has just lost his job, it also turns out to be positive, because now you have time to deal with the family.
I can also feel good - I can predict the end of American drama quite well!
To be honest, the film is well constructed, all the necessary elements are there.
You can cry, you can laugh, the actors are good and the morality is beautiful: money is not the most important thing in life.
I'm sure this movie brings in well.
Trump's new chief of staff plans to curb the president's media consumption.
Others have tried and failed.
Maybe John F.Kelly can actually do it.
In that case, he'd be the first.
Politico writes that the White House's new chief of staff plans to limit the flow of information that will reach President Trump, including news, in the hope that it will keep the boss in more flat water.
Here's an excerpt from reporter Josh Dawsey:
“When the White House’s new chief of staff, John F. Kelly, met with senior officials on his first working day, he pointed out a key issue at President Donald Trump’s White House that he planned to solve that bad information reached out to the president.
Kelly told officials that the information had to go through her, either on paper or at meetings, because the president would make better decisions if good information was shared with her."
Kelly's diagnosis seems entirely logical, but others have also tried it, and they've faileded to tame Trump by tracking her media consumption.
President Trump's relationship with television goes back decades, and even now that he's in the White House, his TV-watching habits hasn't gone anywhere.
“When nominee Trump was annoyed with unfair media coverage, it’s worth showing him that he was also featured honestly in the influenced publications,” former campaign adviser Sam Nunberg told Politico in February.
Politico Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that "six former campaigners say Trump's key to managing his Twitter habits is to ensure that his personal media consumption also consistently includes praise."
All right. Okay.
But the idea that Trump's habits of using Twitter have once been "under control" is ridiculous.
Perhaps these campaign workers know something that the rest of us don’t know that Trump’s tweets would have been even more flammable without these interventions.
We'll probably never find out about tweets that Trump didn't post.
When his assistants were able to save him from mischievours even a couple of times, their efforts were worth it.
However, no one has been able to prevent Trump from consistently controversy.
Part of the problem is that at the White House, consisting of clinks competing against each other, people try exclusively to advance their own interests by conveying to Trump material that supports their worldviews, but may not always be reliable.
Politico, covering the entire course of the story, wrote in May about the tendency of advisers to feed information of strategically questionable value to the president.
One of the examples described by reporter Shane Goldmacher is as follows:
“Current and former Trump’s subordinates say that Trump could respond explosively to the negative press, in particular information leaks with adverse effects, as an obsession to identify their source.
That's what happened at the end of February, when someone unfairly handeded to the president a printed copy of an article on GotNews.com's website owned by internet provocateur Charles C. Johnson, which accused Deputy Chief of Staff Katie Walsh that she was the White House's "source of several information leaks."
It doesn't matter that Johnson is permanently blocked from Twitter due to mockery, or that he didn't offer any concrete evidence, or that he's filed false accusations in the past and withdrew.
“Trump read an article and started asking his subordinates about Walsh.”
Goldmacher added that Reince Priebus, the then chief of staff, and Rob Porter, head of the White House Chancellery, "have tried to establish a system for managing and documenting documents reaching Trump."
How did it work?
Three months later, Kelly tries to do exactly the same thing.
He may turn out to be a more effective leader from Priebus, but Trump is still impulsive, and his assistants are still competing for influence.
These unchanging factors make Kelly's mission very difficult.
Street musicians who have come to the capital from abroad are not eager to get permission to perform.
There are a lot of foreigners playing in the centre of Tallinn.
Those with whom Õhtuleht spoke admitted that they are playing in the Old Town without the permission of the city government.
First, we found three street musicians near the Viru gates, who had brought the same number of dogs with them.
It was clearly visible that musicians with dreadlocks and blasting clothes were backpackers, who could use a shower.
Despite his appearance, the group's only woman on the accordion played a song with a fun rhythm, while one man juggled next to him with three balls and invited passers-by closer.
The third man had sat next to them on a backpack and smoked a rolled-up joint in peace of mind.
Their three dogs didn't allow themselves to be disturbed by the passing people.
Bob, who played the accordion with Noem and juggled with balls, said he was playing in the streets for both fun and money.
The purpose of the group's performance was to collect ticket money for a ship sailing to Finland in the evening of the same day.
Asked if they asked the city for permission to make music in the Old Town, Noem and Bob shook their heads.
First of all, we didn't know.
Secondly, we will never stay in one city for more than a day or a couple, and for the most part, permits will be given for a month or something like that.
There's no point in paying for it," Bob explained.
About 20 minutes later, when we ended up again at the Viru Gates, we saw that two women were with the musicians and driving them away.
The group moved with backpacks and dogs to the intersection of Viru Keskus to try their luck.
There were also three Polish young men on Viru Street, one of whom played the violin, the other the clarinet, and the third had carried an electronic piano.
“We’re here to entertain people to spread music and joy and to make money for fuel and living,” said pianist Mivosh.
According to Mivoshi, they are aware that there must be permission from the city government to perform.
"Of course we don't have it," he said jokingly.
But let's hope that if people enjoy our music, there's no problem.
Of course, if the police come, we're going elsewhere, but we have to try," he added.
Eesti Pank bought 149 million euros worth of bonds in July as part of the central banks of the euro area's asset asset, and the central bank's bond portfolio grew to a total of 4.36 billion euros.
The central bank does not disclose more detailed information about purchases.
Hardo Hansson, the Governor of Eesti Pank, has previously explained in response to an interpellation by members of the Riigikogu that the asset purchase programme of the Eurosystem central banks includes three different purchase programmes: covered bonds in the financial sector, asset-backed bonds (ABS) and public sector bonds (PSPP).
The central bank is buying bonds from the market to encourage investors who have sold bonds to invest money elsewhere.
As a result of purchases, the yield of lower-risk assets decreases, which motivates investors to take greater risks.
As a general rule, each central bank buys government bonds from its own country under the public sector bond-buying programme, and bonds from other countries are not purchased.
As the Estonian state has not issued bonds, Eesti Pank makes so-called replacement purchases within the allocated volume.
Replacement purchases are also carried out by central banks in those countries where the supply of bonds corresponding to the purchase programme is limited.
According to Hansson, Eesti Pank generally buys bonds from European institutions, such as the European Financial Stability Facility and the European Investment Bank bonds.
Eesti Pank has been buying bonds as part of the asset purchase programme since March 2015.
In the meantime, Elering's bonds were also acquired, but Eesti Pank will no longer buy Elering's bonds from June 2016.
The central bank of Finland has been buying the bonds of both Elering and Eesti Energia since June on behalf of the central banks of the euro area.
The travelers ignore the ditch and return to the "millionaire district"
Some of West Midland's wealthiest homeowners have once again fallen into fierce confrontation with determined travelers who have ignored the council's dug ditch to win back the field away from their villas.
Migrants have parked their 20 caravans on the field as a prosperous "millionaire district" near Solihull Street.
They have returned with an army of horses, children and trucks after they were knocked out only in May, without being disturbed by a ditch dug by the council to prevent their intrusion.
The tactile group found a weak spot in the guard, squeezing their vehicles through an unsustained part that had been lefted as a footpath.
The furious residents have cursed the Solihull council, saying that the council is "useless in dealing with the problem."
An anonymous resident said: "They moved there Monday night after their expulsion from Bentley Heath, where they threw stones at passers-by.
The council must be embarrassed so that they take action, because they are useless in dealing with the problem.
The travelers were there in May, they were chased away, and now they're back again.
There'd be more last night, probably even more today."
He added: "Birmingham's council knows how to deal with such things by procuring lengthy injunctions to protect their parks.
“I’m living on the Streetbrook route, and I’m not happy that I have to keep paying the council if the council can’t even stop it.”
One of the locals, who also wished to remain anonymous, said: "This is ridiculous. We just saw how the last of these travelers left, and a new offensive is already taking place.
This time the number has increased, and even a horse has been seen ruminant in grassland."
A spokesman for Solihull's council said: "We are aware that a campground is unacceptable in the land near Streetsbrook Road, and we are cooperating with Solihull's police. We will implement the necessary legislative measures to get them moving as soon as possible.
Evidence is very helpful if as many local residents as possible report any antisocial or criminal behavior to the police by calling 101.
This will help to create a picture of the situation for the court if we present the case to them."
Minister of Culture Indrek Saar, among others, will visit the Weekend festival, who is pleased that it has been possible to bring the world's top names to Estonia.
Asked why the Weekend festival is so important for Estonians, Saar replies that it should be asked by all of those who fully enjoy the three-day talk of the festival.
However, he admits that real people in their field have arrived in Pärnu.
"Lookinginginginging the level of artists that have been brought to Estonia, they bring home the absolute top names of this genre of music - this often does not happen in our area," says the Minister of Culture.
