Audi upgrades up to 850 000 diesel vehicles in the midstep of critiques against car manufacturers
A day before the Audi announcement, BMW said it was offering software upgrades for more than 350 000 vehicles.
According to the chairman of BMW, the company believes that "there are smarter options than driving restrictions", citing European cities, including BMW's hometown of Munich, which has considered banning or restricting diesel vehicles.
Daimler said on Tuesday that it will upgrade three million Mercedes vehicles in Europe to reduce emissions from diesel engines.
None of the companies described these actions as a recall.
European automakers have enthusiastically promoted the use of diesel vehicles in Europe and the United States to help comply with the regulations imposed on CO2 emissions.
However, nitric oxide emitted from diesel engines is considered a carcinogen and can cause asthma.
At the same time, the cost of equipment to be installed to neutralise the exhaust gases emitted by diesel vehicles for automotive companies will increase, making it difficult to keep the price of cars competitive.
While German automakers are thoroughly under investigation, Chancellor Angela Merkel's government is also accused of pampering mighty automakers and ignoring the signs of the problem.
Companies are trying to avoid replicating Volkswagen's mistakes, who silenced the use of so-called scammers, which made it possible to adjust the exhaust emission reading to comply with the regulations for the duration of testing of the car, but withdrew under normal driving conditions.
Several Volkswagen drivers have been indicted in the United States and others are under investigation on both sides of the Atlantic.
Last month, the former head of the thermodynamics unit of Audi's engine development department was arrested in Germany.
Zaccheo Giovanni Pamio, a former director, is Italian and is therefore not protected from extradition and could go on trial in the United States.
Earlier this year, Audi was drawn into a criminal investigation into Volkswagen in Germany when the authorities accused the Audit system of installing a system to circumvent European emission requirements, thereby expanding an investigation that had previously focused on the United States.
The inclusion of Audi in the investigation could be a big blow for Volkswagen ₖ the manufacturer of luxury cars generates a disproportionate share of Volkswagen's profits.
European Union flags will be purchased for Estonian schools for 20 000 euros.
The Ministry of Education plans to buy European Union flags for Estonian schools by 1 September.
In total 105x158 centimetres of the European Union's flag, 570 educational institutions.
The flags are intended for indoor display, they must be carried out in high-quality printing equipment indoors also with a tunned mount, ERR's news portal reports.
"Gift to Estonian schools for marking the first Estonian Presidency of the Council of the European Union.
According to the Ministry of Education, the estimated cost of 570 flags is 20 000 euros, i.e. one flag will cost 35 euros.
Journalist Murder Emphasizes Growing Threat Situation in Mexico
Employees of the weekly Riodoce usually gather on Wednesdays to review plans to reflect the most recent chaos in the state of Sinaloa, sown by organized crime, corrupt officials, and incessant drug wars.
But today they have gathered under the guise of a personal tragedy to discuss their own safety.
They're told it's important to change their routines.
Two senior journalists are discussing which seems safer: whether to bring the children into an office that was the target of a grenade attack in 2009, or to leave them at home.
Security experts have written three words on the board at the head of the room: "enemies", "neutral", "allies".
They ask reporters to offer names in each column  no evidence is needed, impressions and intuition are enough.
Drug traffickers, politicians, businessmen and journalists are suspected of being paid by the government or cartels, a whole list of villains who make it dangerous to report the chaos in Mexico.
There is no pause for breath from violence, and the more bodies accumulate all over the country, most of which are made up by journalists. According to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, since President Enrique Pena Nieto's inauguration in December 2012, at least 589 journalists have been under state protection after attacks and threats, and at least seven journalists in seven states have been killed so far this year.
The most recent casualties include their editor and source of inspiration, Javier Valdez Cardenas, co-founder of Riodoce.
"It's the biggest mistake to live in Mexico and be a journalist," Valdez wrote.
His absence is deeply felt, although his presence can be perceived everywhere  On the façade of the Riodoce building, a large picture of Valdez pointing the middle finger hangs with the inscription "Justice", and two 30s reporters, Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez, wear T-shirts depicting his smiling face with glasses or his characteristic panama hat.
The meeting will take place less than two months after his death. Reporters will discuss joint trauma, his nightmares, insomnia, paranoia.
Although a separate Public Prosecutor's Office was set up to handle journalists' cases in 2010, according to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, only two cases have been prosecuted.
As is the case for most annual drug trafficking and organised crime-related murders, which amounts to thousands, journalist killers rarely go to trial.
Riodoce's editorial office will continue to cover the violence in Sinaloa, even though their hearts have been broken and the landscape is now even more dangerous.
Without the murderers' data and justice, a meeting on security will not be of much use, according to Ibarra.
"It's very silly to waste your time in this meeting," he says.
"As long as the reason's not known, you'll distrust everyone."
Valdez left Riodoce's office in Culiacan on the morning of May 15.
He only managed to drive for a few quarters before two men stopped his red Toyota Corolla. He was forced to leave the car and was shot 12 times, presumably because of the name of the newspaper, which translates to "twelfty rivers".
An armed man drove away in Valdez's car and crashed it nearby.
Valdez was 50 years old.
He was left behind by a wife and two grown children.
Rumors spread freely in the Culiacan.
However, there is essentially only silence about Valdez.
Since Guzman's arrest last year and extradited to the United States in January, Sinaloa has been one of the country's bloodiest battlefields, with rival clicks fighting to fill the power vacuum.
It doesn't matter that some people, or even several, are shot every day in the streets of Sinaloa, or that the graveyard's graveyard swells from abundantly decorated mausoleums of double drug kings, which are larger than the homes of many.
Let us forget for a moment for a moment that peacetime refers to how only one cartel leads the seaside state with valuable ports and drug routes to the United States.
Despite all this and the fact that Valdez was constantly aware of the dangers involved in his work, Ismail Bojorquez (60), co-founder and manager of Riodoce, feels the immense remorse that he was unable to protect his friend.
He believes the killing was facilitated by two mistakes.
The first of these was an interview with Damaso Lopez, who competes with Guzman's sons and is at a warfare carteless cliff in February.
This piece of written may have angered the sons that the suspected gang members bought all copies of the publication as soon as they arrived at the news stalls.
Another mistake was that Valdez was not forced to leave the country for his own safety after a raising took place in another newspaper editorial office covering the same story.
Valdez was a legend both in Mexico and abroad, and his assassination is seen as a turning point of violence against journalists in Mexico.
He had survived for so long that his friends and colleagues assumed that he would be there forever.
He worked as a veteran reporter for Noroeste newspaper in 2003 when he joined five colleagues to create a Riodoce, selling 50-dollar units.
Bojorquez said that it was "impossibly impossible to deal with the press without touching the drug problem."
Over time, the newspaper gained notoriety with its courageous and honest coverage, and sales and advertising increased.
Reporters liked to publish vigorous research without fear of censorship, and readers were fascinated by a publication from which they could read stories that no one else dared to cover.
Eight years after the founding of Riodoce, the newspaper won the prestigious Maria Moors Cabot Prize for its Latin American coverage.
That same year, Valdez won the International Press Freedom Award by the Committee for the Protection of Journalists (AKK) for his courage to follow the Mexican drug story wherever it leads.
He openly testified that he was afraid.
"I want to keep living," he said when he received the AKK Prize.
Last October, in an interview with Rompeviento TV, he said that drug trafficking is a way of life in Sinaloa.
"You have to embrace the task that comes with the profession of a journalist ♪ Another way is to play dumb.
“I don’t want to be asked what I did in the face of so many deaths or why I didn’t tell what was going on.”
Riodoce's collective misses Valdez, a joker swearing like a sailor, a friend who kindly shared embraces and advice, a teacher who knew how to survive.
They relied on his routine.
He always wore his hat.
He went from the office across the street to El Guayabo's bar and always sat at the same desk.
Now they ask if his love of routine became fatal to him.
His death has also made them doubt themselves about how best to do their job and stay alived.
"They don't like messing with their wives, their children, their legitimate businesses, their secret landing sites used to transport drugs."
"These things should not be touched," Bojorquez said.
The result, even at the best of times, is strong self-censorship and self-preservation.
Trusting your instincts.
If it smells dubious, stay away.
According to Riodoce's editor Andres Villarreal, the problem is that "the sense of smell can be deceived... and that's what happened to Javier".
He and others say that old rules no longer apply in Sinaloa, just as they don't apply in Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero, or other states, which are home to the toxic community of lucrative smuggling routes, weak institutions, and corrupt government officials.
The landscape is constantly changing.
In a room where Riodoce employees met for security training, cell phones were suddenly no longer allowed; a few days before that, it was revealed that spyware sold exclusively to governments had been used to track journalists and activists in Mexico.
In front of the house, two police officers sought relief from 40-degree heat in the shadow of a tree.
The government ordered them to guard Riodoce's office, which is housed in a four-storey building in the Culiacan's middle-class neighborhood.
Some reporters half-jokingly pondered whether these police officers belonged to those 50 percent of police officers whom the governor himself has labeled unreliable.
Several months have passed since reporters have traveled to mountainous rural areas, where control is in the hands of de facto drug gangs.
Riodoce this week's edition covered three main stories.
One of them was the assassination of former boxing legend Julio Cesar Chavez's brother in Sinaloa.
In addition, they had a revealing story about spending government funds on the governor's hometown.
And a group of people were kidnapped from one of Culiacan's most expensive restaurants a block from the prosecutor's office.
There was no official announcement about who was kidnapped or how it happened, so you had to be careful when it was time to write what everyone in the city knew ※ The restaurant is a favorite place for both drug traders and authorities.
The reporter learned from public documents that the restaurant was registered in the name of a politician who belonged to the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party (IRP), which dominated all levels of politics almost throughout the last century.
More recently, several IRP governors have been accused of corruption in widespread lawsuits.
Villarreal asked the reporter to write about previous incidents in the same place, including how Guzmani's son, nicknamed El Chapo, escaped a military raid.
Just a few months earlier, readers would have been looking for the Valdez column to get information about kidnapping from the best sources.
"In the past, we'd have already knowned what happened," said 46-year-old Villarreal, whose sleek physique has earned him the nickname El Flaco.
"Now all channels of communication with our sources have been interrupted."
Valdez's office has become a storage room for labels and stickers that are protesting against the killing of journalists, and editorial staff has become something they could never have anticipated  activists on behalf of the press.
Reporter Miriam Ramirez grabbed a couple of signs and headed to the local prosecutor's office the next morning for another reporter.
Salvador Adame disappeared three days after Valdez's assassination in the western Michoacan state.
A burnt body has been found, and according to officials, it belongs to him based on DNA tests.
Journalists have become more vocal all over the country and are scribbling the phrase "SOS press" on sidewalks and buildings as an organized protest.
On that day, Bojorquez is away in D.C., trying to gather international support for justice in Valdez's case.
A day after Valdez's death with the governor of Sinaloa, Ramirez accused authorities of snooping on journalists and killing them for telling the truth.
After that, he asked him to be redirected from reflection of the government's activities, worrying that anger had affected his objectivity.
Valdez had repeatedly said Mexican journalists were "limited" by organized crime, complicious civil servants and indifferent societies.
In his latest book "Narco Journalism," he wrote that reporters are not only killed by drug gangs, but also on the orders of politicians and security forces who are involved in organized crime.
Last year, more than half were attributed to police and government officials by the media surveillance group Articulo 19.
"You're going to die in Mexico because you're going to be silenced," Ramirez said.
Murders are obviously bleeding.
No one forgets the death of blogger Maria Elizabeth Macias in Tamaulipas, the northern border state, six years ago.
His body was found with a letter probably signed by cartel Los Zetas: "I'm here because of my reports."
Next to his deflected head was a computer keyboard and headphones.
Some publications have decided to close their doors, such as El Norte in the northern border state of Chihuahua after their correspondent Miroslava Breachi was killed in March.
Others continue to shoot as El Manana de Nuevo Laredo did after the murder of their director in 2004.
In 2010, Diario de Ciudad Juarez openly addressed Narcotics Cartels with "What do you want from us?" on the front page.
Some journalists have escaped from their home states, or even the country.
It's a nerve-wracking decision.
It's hard to find work in exile, and they still have to take a look at the streets in order to avoid the dangers.
And they are sometimes chased, as probably happened to photographer Ruben Espinosa, who was murdered with four women in a Mexican apartment three months after fleeing Veracruz in 2015.
Those who stay put and continue to work have to dance around risky decisions every day.
Ibarra, who once wanted to become a poet, admits that the coverage of the drug business frightens him.
"Mexico's on its way to hell, and that's why I became a reporter," he said.
At the midnight of a recent Friday, when the latest number had already been locked, Riodoce's editors sat on the sidewalk in front of the office and sipped beer when all of a sudden everyone's phones started vibrating.
There were 19 suspects, according to confirmed firefighting between rival gangs and security forces in the coastal resort town of Mazatlan.
The war continued to escalate, as several messages left by cartels found in the area promised.
From there, from the sidewalk edge, reporters posted the news using a mobile phone on Riodoce's website.
The next day's front cover needed to be changed.
Sirens swarmed along ▖ another firefight in the area.
Bojorquez peeked at the officers on duty to see if they were vigilant.
If they were afraid, at least they didn't show it.
Under a massive portrait of the newspaper's fallen founder, on which his middle finger can be seen all over Sinaloa, Riodoce's employees followed in his footsteps.
"How can we even think about closing," Bojorquez said, "when the same day Javier was killed, an intern asked me to send him to the streets for reporting?"
Dinosaurus used a protective color to prevent enemies
At first glance, it looks like a statue, although quite scary.
Scientists have now unveiled the first findings in connection with an incredible rhinocerous-size creature discovered by a Canadian miner that is so well preserved that experts have described it as the "Mona Lisa of dinosaurs."
It's so well-preserved that it was even possible to detect that the skin covered with armor had a reddish-brown color, and his past had been somewhat "complicated".
The report, published in the journal Current Biology, described the existence as "the best-preserved armored dinosaur that has ever been found" and "one of the best dinosaur specimens in the world."
Fossil is a new species of nodosaurus that lived in the middle of the Cretaceous period, 110-112 million years ago.
Its incredibly good preservation bogged down researchers who describe it as "really significant."
According to National Geographic magazine, this armoured vegetarian is the best-preserved fossil of this type that has ever been found.
It was found on March 21, 2011 by Shawn Funk when he mined the Suncor Millenium mine near Fort McMurray in North Alberta, Canada.
He caught something that did not resemble the surrounding stone and decided to take a closer look.
The discovered fossil was dispatched to the Royal Tyrrell Paleontology Museum.
For the next six years, they were working to excavate an animal from a 2500-pound (1100-pound) piece of land.
After all this hard work, the end result is now finally ready to be made public.
"If you squint a little, you can almost believe he'll fall asleep," said lead author Caleb Brown, a researcher at the Royal Tyrrell Museum where the creature is exhibited.
"It goes into the history of science as one of the most beautiful and best-preserved dinosaur specimens - the Mona Lisana of dinosaurs."
Examinating his skins, the researchers found that this plant-eater, although covered in scales and reminiscent of a walking tank, was likely to be significantly threatened by carnivorous dinosaurs.
This dinosaur with many scales used red and white protective colors to hide from predators, and applied a shading technique called counter-eclipse, which is also used by many of today's animals.
According to experts, this allowed the dinosaur to blend better into the surroundings and avoid enemies, as larger dinosaurs were likely to hunt him.
This 110-million-year-old creature belonging to the Nodosaurus family made it to the news for the first time in May and is now visited at the Royal Tyrrell Paleonthology Museum.
Researchers have now explored the creature and named it after Mark Mitchell, a laboratory technician at the Borealopelta Markmitchell Museum, who was part of a team that spent more than 7000 hours uncoveringing an animality.
Due to the astonishably good preservation of the specimen, Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists were able to document the shape and size of the scales on the animal's body.
They discovered a counter-eclipse - a method of protection common in the animal kingdom, which means that the top half of the creature was darker than the lower one.
Although counter-eclipse is prevalent, the findings come as a surprise, as Borealopelta is much larger than today's eclipsed animals.
This suggests that the dinosaur was actively hunted by other carnivorous dinosaurs, that evolution favored hiding as a survival tactic instead of confrontation.
However, most of today's anti-eclipse animals — such as deer, zebras, or armadiles — are much smaller and more vulnerable as prey, suggesting that the nodosaurus had to really struggle to survive.
"The vigorous hunting of a dinosaur covered in huge and abundant scales shows how dangerous the Cretacean dinosaur predators could have been," Brown said.
A chemical analysis of scale organic compounds also revealed that the dinosaur's skin was reddish-brown.
Scientists will continue to study the animal to get clues about its life, including the preserved contents of its gut, to find out what the animal ate for its last meal.
They believe that the dinosaur crashed into the river when it died, thereby reaching the sea with the flow, where it sanked its back to the surface of the ocean.
At that time, Alberta was as warm as it is today in southern Florida, and rivers and oceans probably extended much further inland than they are today.
"This nodosaurus is truly notable, as it is completely covered with preserved scaly leather, while it has also remained three-dimensional, retaining its original shape," Brown said.
"As a result, an animal today looks almost the same as it did in the Cretaceous period.
You don't have too much imagination to reconstruct it - it's enough just to squint your eyes, and it seems like he's asleep."
The Chalk age was a time when huge carnivorous dinosaurs - theropodes - were wandering on the Earth.
While all of them, King Tyrannosaurus rex lived millions of years later than Borealopelta, this armored dinosaur may have been hunted by some of its terrifying predecessors.
These include Acrocanthosaurus, 38 feet (11.5 meters) tall and six-ton monster.
The researchers, whose recent findings have been published in the journal Current Biology, believe Borealopelta was washed into the sea after its death, and it mummified in mud.
The creature was discovered on March 21, 2011 by Shawn Funk when he mined at the Fort McMurray mine near Millenium in North Alberta, Canada.
According to the museum, it is the best-preserved armour dinosaur in the world, including leather and scales, and it is a complete muzzle up to the hips.
These creatures were on average 18 feet (five meters) long and weighed up to 3000 pounds (1300 kg).
Two 20-inches long squirrel penetrated its shoulders.
Scientists believe this armor-covered plant-eater adventured through the current Western Canada until an overfloowed river washed into its open sea.
However, the undersea burial site of the dinosaur helped to preserve its armores in great detail.
The fossilized remnants of this particular specimen are so well-preserved that lumpy armoured plates along the dinosaur skulls are still covered by skin remnants.
As Michael Greshko wrote in National Geographic: "Such a degree of preservation is as rare as winning the lottery.
The more I look at it, the more incredible it seems.
Petrified remnants still cover lumpy armoured plates on the animal's skull.
The right front leg lies next to it, and five toes are extended upwards.
I can count the scales on his sole."
How did an activist find an answer to the question "Whose Streets?"
On August 9, 2014, Brittany Ferrell suffered a complete shock.
Just a day after traveling to New York, this resident of St. Louis sat on a bed in a rented room via Airbnb, browsing Facebook on his phone.
He came across a post from his former schoolmate.
It said: "The police just killed an 18-year-old teenager, and he's still lying on the street."
He was confused about why no one else had posted this information on his feed, which is why he closed Facebook and opened Twitter.
Guided @TheePharoah user posts were repeatedly shared on his timeline.
A rapper from the St. Louis area delivered a real-time tweet about Michael Brown's unarmed 18-year-old black young man, who was killed by a white police officer in Ferguson, Missouri.
He posted a picture of Brown's lifeless body lying on the street, staying there for four hours.
"It's crazy," Ferrell said.
However, death was nothing new for him or his community.
He put away his phone, but later saw tweets again in the evening, which this time announced the gathering of people on Canfield Drive.
They shared photos of police tape and screaming people and a man who claimed to be Brown's father and kept a sign with the inscription "Ferguson's police just killed my unarmed son!"
He watched a live stream in which a policeman stood in front of a group of protesters with a barking dog.
In the second video, another policeman, according to him, picked up a gun and shouted, "I'm going to kill all of you... animals."
Ferrell remembers how he caught himself thinking: "It's unusual."
"It's not the kind of murder that we're used to and what we might call typical."
At that moment, I felt the creeps.
“I was angry and crying.”
He booked a return flight and drove home the next day.
Having landed at 23 o'clock, he went to his father's house to pick up his six-year-old daughter and decided that they would go to Ferguson the next morning.
What happened to Ferrell and Ferguson over the next three years is the central theme in the new documentary "Whose Streets?"), which premieres in cinemas on August 11.
It is the story of survival and protest, love and loss, strength and toughness from the point of view of those people who live in this community and who filled the streets demanding answers.
When Ferrell with his daughter - who was wearing a floral dress and a suitable crown in his head - drove towards Ferguson, they talked again again about what they had talked about many times before this morning - about the experiences and living conditions of black people.
"Did you remember when I taught you how a black community had to fight for their rights?" he recalls asking himself.
"We're driving to Ferguson right now because police killed an 18-year-old boy and that's not right."
"I couldn't not take him," Ferrell said later.
"What's going on right now is the culmination of what has happened in the past.
He needs to know and see it and grow in the midst of it.
He must learn about activism and organization, as well as knowing the different layers of black experiences and living conditions, and what we need to do in order to become free and liberate ourselves.
Even if I manage to raise her in such a way that she doesn't personally experience blattant racism, she's no different from another black girl.
“I feel like I’d do him a disservice by protecting him from this world.”
The first stop they made when they reached Ferguson was on South Florissant's road - a place across the road from the police station, which had become a kind of campground where activists were waiting for news about Officer Darren Wilson, who was not ultimately charged.
Ferrell and his daughter joined forces with a local business owner who prepared lunches.
They put together grocery bags and helped share them with the protesters.
For Ferrell, a new type of activism slowly began to take shape.
"My activism changed completely after August 9," he said, noting that when he was a student at the University of Missouri-St. Louis, he was president of the Minorities Nursing Association and focused on food justice and health-related inequalities in black communities.
"I didn't have any organizing experience.
It all just worked out.”
Weeks later, Ferrell met one of the film's co-directors, Sabaah Folayan.
Folayan said he and filmmaker Lucas Alvarado-Farrar just wanted to document what was going on.
Ferrell's head question arose: "Are you trying to document what's going on or finding a story you can take advantage of?"
Such skepticism was due to countless cases in which people—often white people—come to the community and try to take advantage of its pain and endurance.
Folayan explained in a recent interview: "We had heard that the voices of people born and raised in Ferguson would not be heard, and we wanted to do it differently."
Folayan had already shaken hands with co-director Damon Davis, an artist in the region known for activism in relation to prisoners on death row.
His involvement made the project captivating for Ferrell and six other locals who were watched for years after Brown's shooting.
"In this documentary, no one speaks for us or with us, but we speak ourselves," Davis said.
"It was important to me how these people are depicted, because that's how I'm depicted."
While the goal wasn't to focus on Ferrell's experience - it was intended to show that the movement wasn't a "redeemer guide, it's a community reunion," as Folayan said - he became one of the film's most open part.
As a result, "Whose Streets?" documents surprisingly personal aspects of Ferrell's life, such as falling in love and marrying his wife.
"I felt like I was naked," Ferrell said, laughinginging for the first time he saw the finished film.
"But I realized that doing this work and humanizing black people must be accompanied by a degree of vulnerability."
Moreover, Ferrell's story emphasizes and re-puts the role of a black homosexual woman in the wider Black Lives Matter movement.
Two of the three initiators of the movement, who came up with #BlackLivesMatter on social medato in 2012 following the killing of black teenager Trayvon Martin, are homosexual.
"Gymosexual women, who play a leading role, have kept the movement in general," Ferrell said, "precisely because we have to constantly fight on many fronts.
“Black homosexual women have to carry all this burden.”
Folayan agreed, noting that "the movement is indeed kept alive by black women, and many of them are black homosexual women."
At the same time, he argues that "this is not some kind of equal opportunity-creating thing" - Ferrelli was not chosen as the centerpiece of the film because of his identity.
"He was a person who exuded the right energy," he said.
"But it's not a coincidence that he was directly on the front line, because living in such nodules as a black homosexual woman is at stake.
Its energy can be perceived through the screen."
At the same time, news cameras from Ferguson have long since disappeared, but racial ties between the community and the police are still fragile.
Parologium sentences imposed on Ferrell during the demonstration will continue for another 2.5 years.
He describes the socio-political atmosphere in the area he calls home, just a "huge danger."
Nevertheless, he still attaches importance to the mantra he has etched into memory from the time he went to the streets of Ferguson three years ago.
"It is our duty to fight for our freedom.
It's our duty to win.
We must love and support each other.
We have nothing to lose but our chains.”
7 Ways to Overcome a Hangover After a Weekend Party
Some of the stronger male sons will find out after a drink-absorbed party what's wrong with it, and what helps.
Or proceed from the saying "avoid a hangover, stay drunk".
But this is only an inevitable postponement and a decent bump for health.
What can you do to feel better in the morning after today's Weekend party or any other party tomorrow and get over the hangover as painlessly as possible?
Drinking water.
Under and after alcohol, after a big party in the morning ... or, if possible, even instead of alcohol.
Since the body loses a lot of fluid when it consumes alcohol, it has to be recovered somehow.
Along with water, minerals are also flushed out of the body, so it is good to drink mineral water.
Since the stomach is often irritated after drinking, it is especially good if it is bubble-free or only slightly carbonated.
Why don't you go for a walk.
Certainly, you shouldn't go to Grandma's house with a hangover to break trees - this could turn out to be a fatal misjudgment.
But an easy walk helps the body to remember what it's like to be alive again.
Lying in a tent and sleeping out a hangover is a more time-consuming way to get well.
Given energy back to your body gradually and carefully.
If you're already vomiting from yesterday's overdrinking, it's definitely not worth trying to load a big fat steak into your stomach.
For example, start with the fresh juice, which contains fruit sugars that quietly invigorate your body.
If you already suffer from health around noon, you might want to try broth, for example.
And a little later, a squirrel.
Avoid coffee.
This diuretic frees your body from all water.
As already said, water and juice are your friends.
Don't bet on painkillers.
If you take a painkiller in the evening to prevent a hangover, your liver will have to deal with paracetamol or ibuprofen in addition to alcohol and thus lose performance.
In this way, you'll ruin your lives even more, and from now on, hangovers will be even worse
In addition, the painkiller does not stay in the body for very long anyway.
If you take a painkiller in the morning, don't do it on an empty stomach - it irritates your already ragged stomach.
Or if you don't have a partner, get along well with yourself.
It's pretty much the same as walking, but you don't have an orgasm when you're walking.
However, avoid too active pace and crazy postures - during a hangover, physical exertion should be moderate.
Make a joke.
Talk with your friends and fellow sufferers, do simple stupid things and laugh amicably over yourself and others (sufferings).
Joke and laughter may not drive the hangover away, but it's definitely more fun to endure the follow-ups.
Once upon a time, a hangover goes away anyway.
Northwestern professor and University of Oxford employee are being sought as suspects in murder
Police said a Northwestern University professor and an employee of the University of Oxford is being hunted nationwidally, who has become suspects after finding the body of a man with deadly stabbing wounds in a luxury apartment in Chicago.
According to Cook's county court documents, arrest warrants were handededed Monday without the possibility of bail for the arrest of 42-year-old Wyndham Lathem and 56-year-old Andrew Warre, on first-degree murder for their alleged involvement in Trenton H. James Cornell-Duranleau's death.
Cornell-Duranleau, 26, died after repeated stabbing.
According to a warning issued by the Chicago Police Department to the community, the body was discovered on July 27.
Officers received a phone call and, when they arrived at the apartment building in the Grand Plaza, found a victim with several stab wounds on his body.
Officials said Cornell-Duranleau was declared dead at the scene, and investigators treated stabbing as murder, while police are actively searching for two suspects.
Lathem has worked as an associate professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007.
Alan K. Cubbage, the university's vice president of public relations, said in an email that Lathem has now been temporarily suspended and has been banned from entering Northwestern University campuses.
"This is now an investigable criminal case investigated by the competent authorities, and Northwestern University is cooperating with investigators," Cubbage said.
Warren is a Senior Assistant in the Finance Department at Oxford University.
A spokeswoman from the University and Somerville College said in an e-mail statement: "We have been in contact with the British police and are ready to assist U.S. investigative agencies in any way possible."
In addition, the statement said Warren's colleagues were shocked and wanted him to extradite himself.
Anthony Guglielmi, head of the communications department of the Chicago Police Department, urges the suspects to surrender themselves to authorities.
"The police have an idea of where they are, but we are working more and more intensively in defining the exact location," Guglielmi said.
According to Guglielm, local, regional and national authorities are helping to look for suspects.
Guglielmi said the police received a phone call from the administrator of the Grand Plaza apartment building, who had just received a cryptic hint that something had happened in apartment 1008.
According to police descriptions, this was a gruesome sight.
According to Guglielm, it is not clear whether the event itself also took place on July 27.
He said security cameras confirm Lathem's and Warren's presence in the building.
"We believe Professor Latham and the victim had a relationship," Guglielmi added.
"The police are currently working on a chronology of events and figuring out the victim's background, and is exploring various motives, including the possibility of a family dispute."
startups that can help your business avoid becominging the next Uber
Amy Errett wanted to measure employee satisfaction in her e-commerce start-up, but the polls were revenueless.
The answers were often vague, useless or, even worse, misleading.
And even if he promised to ensure anonymity, some employees did not trust this process.
"This system simply wasn't consistent and objective," said Errett, who manages Madison Reed, an e-commerce company that sells hair care products based in San Francisco with 75 employees.
So he called on outsiders to help.
Start-ups with new types of human resources are emerging to help companies figure out how their employees are feeling.
By developing and licensing software with the specific goal of measuring employee engagement, they allow companies to conduct sudden surveys, target specific teams and demographic groups, provide anonymity to employees and a hot line for complaints, and, in some cases, allow language carriers to bypass C-level executives and go directly to the management board.
"Now there are tools like Strava and Fitbit designed to monitor your health, but where's Fitbit for your company?" asked Jim Barnett, co-founder and CEO of Redwood City start-up Glint, whose software analytics tools are used by companies to measure employee engagement.
According to Errett, in the three years he has used Glint, he has gained a much better overview of what his employees think and feel.
In addition to sudden surveys and surveys aimed at specific teams, it offers a heat map of the company, which gives an overview of which departments have the most complaints and which managers have the lowest support, allowing them to find out the reasons.
Companies are beginning to realize that they need to stay up to date with their work culture in order not to become the next Uber that has been wrapped up in scandals since a former employee published a blog post describing a harassing environment punishing those who dared to speak out.
For start-ups like Glint, having an overview request is a lucrative business opportunity.
The global area of management, regulation and compliance may be worth more than US$118.7 billion by 2020, according to data from the financial technology statistics website Let's Talk Payments.
Convercent, a Colorado-based start-up in Denver to help companies prevent and detect inappropriate behavior, spotted an increase in interest and activity against the backdrop of Uber's dubious reputation this year.
Convercent has almost 600 customers, including Airbnb, Microsoft and Tesla.
Uber recently registered as a customer.
Like Glintki, Convercent lets companies send custom impulse surveys, collect confidential responses in real time, and view heat maps from problem areas.
It also provides an anonymous messaging line that allows employees to report inappropriate behaviour.
And when it comes to the CEO, the complaints go directly to the management board.
"Public opinion has been snatched by regulators," said Patrick Quinlan, founder and CEO of Convercent.
Quinlan said that if a company is found to treat its employees poorly or behaves unethically, the company could face serious consequences, such as consumer boycotts, employee departures and lawsuits, even if regulators do not intervene.
Ruby Tuesday, a restaurant chain with more than 25,000 employees on 500 sites, has been using Convercent for more than a year to ensure employee awareness of regulations and operations and provides an easily way of interacting with corporate headquarters.
If an employee previously wanted to report the problem, he had to find a phone number or e-mailing address at the corporation's headquarters, file a formal complaint, and hope that it would be taken seriously.
It was often a daunting and inconvenient process, Ruby Tuesday's general adviser James Vitrano said there wasn't a good way to track employee complaints.
However, now that the company is using Convercent, problems such as discrimination, harassment, or dishonest wage practices that did not previously reach executives who operated in offices in other cities or states can be more quickly identified and dealt with.
"I will be able to get closer to the employee's holistic 360-degree experience," said Vitrano, who leads the Ruby Tuesday risk management group.
"And we will protect our shareholders from litigation with employees."
Quinlan said the companies began to take ethical behavior, values and employee involvement much more seriously in 2002, that is, after Arthur Andersen, an auditing firm, crashed due to ethical violations in connection with the Enron scandal.
However, only when "social media matured," companies realized that they couldn't prevent their dirty laundry from being washed openly online.
"Before using technology to monitor ethical behavior, people used hope as a strategy," he said.
Both Glint and Convercent offer their software as a service, charging companies a periodic fee for using their products.
It's a business model and an opportunity with the approval of venture capital investors who have been supporting both start-ups.
Convercent was funded in February by companies such as Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital for US$10 million.
Glint was invested 10 million dollars by Bessemer Venture Partners in November, increasing the company's total funding to 60 million dollars.
Such investments will not come as a surprise when it comes to taking into account the interrelvencies between companies, culture and venture capital.
More and more research shows that today’s employees expect more from the workplace than before.
Competitive markets such as Silicon Valley have high salaries and interesting projects are merely stakes on the table.
Employees want to feel that they are accepted and valued and that they devote their time to a company that has a positive mission.
"When people are happy in the company, they feel listened to, and that the work they do is rewarding, they are more committed to ensuring the company's success," said Nina McQueen, who is vice president of the General Benefit and Employee Experiencing Division on LinkedIn, which uses both Convercent and Glint.
Investors earn income when companies in their portfolio are doing well; companies are doing well when employees are committed and engaged.
If third-party analytics tools promise to increase employee engagement and engagement, it's no wonder they're getting support.
According to experts in work culture, having data on the involvement of employees is important.
However, this data is useless if the company's supervisors don't start anything with them.
In fact, when a company asks for feedback from its employees, it can create expectations that there will be changes.
And if changes don't happen fast enough or not at all, it can lead to frustration and reduce employee engagement.
"If you ask for 4000 proposals, you have to be ready for 4000 conversations," said Russell Raath, president of Kotter International, a consulting and business management company who has seen how companies make the mistake of relying too much on data collection.
"Because if things don't come to an end, the employees will think, "Did you actually listen to me?
Do you care?
And if you don't care, then why should I care?"
According to Errett, Madison Reed, who now conducts monthly surveys among the employees, has been able to implement measures on the same day that the problem has been reported through Glint.
And after receiving feedback from employees who were disappointed with the speed of decision-making and the quality of communication, he was able to rearrange several teams in the company and offer communication training to deal with the problems.
Ruby Tuesdays, however, collects new employee data, and it is hoped that in the long term this will lead to a longer-term commitment of employees in an industry known for its high staff turnover.
"If you're not committed to ensuring transparency, you're losing people," Vitrano said.
"And if you lose people, you lose customers.
And if you lose customers, you'll lose businesses."
The glittering star of the euro area is the Baltics: incomes are growing rapidly
Although the 2008 crisis and trade problems with Russia have had an impact on the Baltics, the three Baltic countries are rapidly approaching the incomes of the rich countries of Western Europe, Bloomberg writes, citing a report by the European Central Bank.
According to the central bank's data, since 1999, the total production of the economy per capita in Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania has exceeded expectations.
Success is driven by low indebtedness, rapidly recovering institutions and business- and free market-friendly policies.
"Baltic countries, along with Slovakia, are among the few countries in the euro area where real GDP per captive purchasing power has significantly approached the EU average," the central bank said in a statement.
Although the goal is such that the total output of the economy per person would be equal to a richer Western Europe, in 2015 the total output of the Baltic economies per capita accounted for only 2/3 of the average of 15 countries that belonged to the European Union before the 2004 round of expansion.
Low wages have led the Baltics to look for better wages abroad.
Even though the European Central Bank sees the Baltics in a positive light and the economy is also growing this year, local residents are not enthusiastic at all.
According to the most recent Eurobarometer survey, only a quarter of the population in Latvia and Lithuania estimated that their economies were doing very well or well.
In the Czech Republic and Poland, slightly less than half of those surveyed thought this about the economies of their countries.
The NCAA's decision to annul twice-daily workouts makes Bruins days long
It's almost 23 o'clock, when a bunch of UCLA footballers stop to chat on the edge of a new practice field on the team's campus.
Some of them have peeled off their shirts, exposing the sweaty back from the efforts of the first day of their training camp.
For a large part of the team, these idle minutes offer a midday break.
Players attending the summer courses will return to the dormitory after a three-hour workout to study for final exams before returning to the Wasserman football centre for meetings at 6:30 a.m. the next morning.
"The next three days will be a real challenge for them," Bruins coach Jim Mora said shortly before Monday became Tuesday.
The NCAA's decision to cancel twice-daily workouts to reduce players' burdens has actually made their days longer - at least until the final exam is finished on Friday.
For this reason, UCLA's first three training sessions were scheduled to begin at 19:15 a.m
Switching from two workouts a day means more days with one workout - Bruins training camp started almost a week ahead compared to the previous season, and for the first time during Mora's six Westwood seasons, workouts take place during summer courses.
They are scheduled until August 20 for 18 workouts before formal preparation for the opening game against Texas A&M in September begins.
Mora says he's in favor of the NCAA's policy change, although it adds another agenda item to players' tight charts.
"They have a huge load in three or four days, and that's a serious challenge," Mora said.
"And they will break through this ordeal; they will make an effort, they will be nice young people.
I believe that when Saturday arrives and we have more time, it will be truly enjoyable."
When the camp is over, UCLA's training is closed, which is by no means an easy task, taking into account a clear view of their squares from a nearby parking lot and the adjacent Luskin Center, a high-end hotel that has already been hit with footballs that have survived the defensive net around the goalpost.
Starting this weekend, Bruins players have been occupying the hotel, but Mora admitted that there may be problems with available rooms during the season.
"We're implementing security measures," Mora said.
"It's a real concern and it's not exaggerated to think that people can rent a room and film a workout from there.
If you're having a recording of a workout, it can make a pretty good deal.
But we're using a security team there and parking lot 8.
Let's do our best when using what's available.
I'm trying not to worry much about it.
We have a plan in place.”
