Washington's changing the rules of the green card
Media outlets cite President Donald Trump, who said a new U.S. immigration plan is planned that will change the rules of the green card.
The plan provides for a reduction in the number of legal immigrants to 50 000 people per year, stricter rules for obtaining temporary working visas and restrictions regarding family-oriented visas.
New rules have been proposed by Republicans, and the president supports them.
The bill will be debated in the two chambers of Congress.
"This league will reduce poverty, raise wages and save billions of dollars of taxpayers' money by changing the way the U.S. issues green cards to citizens of other countries.
The law will reduce chain migration and replace the current programme.
The new application process gives an advantage to candidates who speak English and who have the financial resources to support themselves and the skills that will benefit our economy," Trump said.
Young people in Siberian Seto are moving to the city, pensioners are moving back to the countryside.
Yevseyev's Anatoly continues the tradition of the famous Seto gold miners along the former Maana River.
He works 140 kilometers from his house on the Maana tributarious ridge by Kuvai as a driver in a gold artel.
It's hard work: two months in a row without days off 12 hours of work, and then only ten days at home and back again.
During his three years of working at Artell, Anatoly has only seen gold once.
"There's a hard order: when you go inside, you check thoroughly so you don't bring vodka, and when you go out, so you don't take gold with you," says Anatoly.
Mare Piho is talking in the village of Haida to the local Seto Jaan, who has told him a lot about her family's memories in Siberia.
The archaic culture of Siberian Setos, which is still preserved, is also beginning to affect domestic Setos, because it is just so fabulous, Piho Mare, who studied the distant Setos most profoundly.
His first research trip to Siberia this year is 30 years ago.
During this time, he has been there 14 times and has lived there for a total of one year.
How many Setus still migrated to Siberia at the end of the 19th and early 20th centuries to get land here?
It's an unresolved issue.
I'm suggesting, 8000.
I figured it out according to Siberian Seto men's stories, who themselves have thought about it.
It's hard to say exactly, because up to 30 people could come in one group at the time, but the passport was only given to a man.
The passport was given only to one, but he had children, sisters and other female relatives.
See how big the territory was here.
It was many times bigger than the historical Setomaa.
It had to be educated and managed in some way.
Setos came here like peasants, but their lives changed, because here they ended up in areas that were not only related to agriculture, but also with the river (log rafting), gold mines, industry.
For example, in New Pechory, many Setos later went to work in the classified city Krasnoyarsk-45 (there the Soviet power built a uranium enrichment plant - J.P.) when it was formed.
Did the Setos make up the majority of the population in this region at the time?
Yeah, they lived compactly.
In addition, the tradition of marrying with each other and many children were preserved for a long time.
Seven or eight children in the family were normal.
How much did the culture of Siberian Setos differ and differ from that of the Setos in the motherland?
They're different, even though what's been brought here used to be the same there.
They brought a lot of women's clothes.
They also made their own (folk clothes), but they always made the difference between what had been brought and what had been done here.
Climate change had a strong impact on Seto's traditional clothing, because it's colder here.
Meals have changed.
For example, in Setomaa the meat was salted, but here it was simply frozen.
Where are Siberian Seto villages and what kind of life is inhabited there? / graphics: Alari Paluots
The connection with the motherland was mentally strong, it was considered very important.
It seems to me that now this Setumaa is going to give it back there, starting to affect them.
Those girls who have lived here in families have listened to amazing stories.
Piho refers to the people of the Seto delegation in Estonia, who came to Haida at the beginning of July and lived for four days at homes - J. P.
What's left here is fabulous.
"It's extremely easy to come to Tapa and also leave, because the city has a great location: at the railway junction and the intersection of highways," says Reigo Tamm, an opera soloist from Tapa.
The city has long ceased to be just the military capital, we have a lot of nature, sports and cultural venues, and you can get pizza on every corner.
Tapa's a top city!
There is a small island by the Valgejõgi River that runs through the city, where killers tend to have a Midsummer party.
"You can't see this beautiful place from the train window," Reigo muzzles.
Those who are not afraid of cold water can swim in this river, because the river is full of springs.
I’ve been swimming there, it’s okay!
On the edge of the river, you can have a picnic and just make romance - you can look at the flow of the river and think about life in advance and in the back.
I've been there with my brides, too.
In addition to everything else on the riverbank, Tamm has been practicing his aries between three bridges: "Since this place is on the outskirts of the city, no one may meet me if I hump my own stories".
"There is a wonderful view of Tapa from Männikumäe, but there are also health trails, where you can run, ski in the winter and now, for example, play disc golf - the golf course is done, discs can be rented from the fitness center," Reigo directs.
Of course, old pros go there with their discs, but I haven't been there yet.
You will probably have to invite my colleague Rasmus Kull to visit Tapa, because he is an experienced disc golfer and he has discs.
Maybe he'll lend me!
Reigo and Männikumägi were inseparable during the childhood of the current opera soloist.
At least during skiing hours.
There are many tears and disappointments left on this mountain, and wins in my sports life.
Not to mention broken sleds and skis.
"The whole city centre is currently full of construction work, all of this is changing beyond recognition," says Reigo.
Tapa's old and tired station building, however, is a very attractive place where summer productions are played.
I am glad that Märt Meos, the manager of R.A.A.A.Mi in 2012, accepted my idea and came to the station with his theatre to make summer theatre.
Although this year's theatre season with "Aleksei Karenin" has just ended, on 11 August there will be "Resurrection" at the railway station, when people move around in the 1980's in the old station building.
"From 11 to 13 August, there will be Tapa City Days, in the framework of which open home cafés of the station building have awakened and a series of cultural events will take place, including a folk rally," says Reigo, who recommends visiting the 91-year-old city these days.
There has been a museum in a small yellow cabin on May 1-May since 1994.
"The house is small, but there are a lot of exhibits and a huge amount of information," Reigo praises the place, which is filled with the history of the city of Tapa, most of which focuses on railways and the military.
Reigo smiles that if you go through all the recommended places in Tapa, you will get hungry, but luckily the small town is full of cafés.
"We have such good places to eat, and when you're sitting in cafés, you sometimes feel like you're abroad," he describes.
Educated and young soldiers who come to Tapa spend a lot of time in pubs and cafés, so sitting there is a strange feeling that you are in Estonia and your hometown, but there is such an international community all around.
I think it would be very exciting to listen to stories from the French or the Americans.
Soldiers, yes, there's a lot we're moving!
Two Bulgarians arrested in Greece for illegal camping
TV channel bTV quoted the foreign ministry, which announced that two Bulgarians had been arrested for illegal camping in Greece.
On the morning of August 1, police arrested a group of illegally camped people on the Sithonia Peninsula, including two Bulgarians.
They were released the same day, but there's an investigation right now.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs told bTV that the Bulgarians were later released.
The Greek authorities have increased restrictions on free camping and are already imposing heavy fines on anyone who is caught vacationing in a place that is not intended for it.
Inspections will continue throughout the season.
According to the ministry, the court may impose a fine.
Wolves teaching puppies break sheep.
The farmers of Hiiumaa again have to give "educational material" to the wolves after a pause after a pause - probably wolves who have taught puppies have already killed a number of sheep.
This year, wolves in Hiiu counties have killed sheep on three occasions, a total of 23 sheep, and eight have been injured, writes Hiiu Leht.
Last year's number was only three, a year earlier 124.
Data from the Environmental Agency is also in sync with the fractures that no wolf litters were born in Hiiumaa last year and information from the Hiiumaa Hunting Council that this year there have been two wolf litters fixed on the island.
Last Monday, wolves broke 15 sheep in Kabuna village in Emmaste rural municipality and injured half of 18 surviving sheep.
Early Wednesday morning, wolves broke a sheep in the pasture of Reheselja village and badly injured the other, to whom an emergency killing had to be made.
The rules for the handling of genetically modified crops must be clear
The Ministry of Rural Affairs sent the Bill on Amendments to the Genetically Modified Organism Release Act (GMOVS) and the Plant Protection Act.
The amendment to the Act abandons the creation of a separate database for both genetically modified (GM) crop locations and handlers, and the plant health register will be used for this purpose in the future.
In addition, the obligation of the GM crop operator to have a prior agreement within the prescribed radius with a person engaged in beekeeping will be amended.
Although genetically modified crops are not currently grown in Estonia, we consider it necessary to have clear rules for the coexistence of genetically modified and non-modified crops.
At least until it has been decided to declare Estonia GMO-free," said Sigmar Suu, head of the plant health department of the Ministry of Rural Affairs.
"At the same time, it must be taken into account that the prepared measures must be purposeful and proportionate, taking into account the costs and administrative burdens related to both the private and public sectors," Suu added.
The Bill repeals the provisions related to the GM crop locations and handlers' database and handling training in GMOVS, establishes the requirements for the handling of GM crops and the commencement of economic activities.
With regard specifically to apiculture, the current obligation of the GM crop grower to inform beekeepers of the commencement of the cultivation of the GM crop will be replaced by the obligation to reach an appropriate agreement with the beekeeper prior to the commencement of cultivation.
The Bill has been sent to ministries for approval.
All interested parties have the opportunity to familiarize themselves with it in the information system and, if they wish, submit their opinion to the Ministry of Rural Affairs.
The planned changes will primarily affect persons, beekeepers and the Agricultural Board operating in the field of crop production.
In the European Union, including Estonia, it is allowed to grow only one GM crop - maize MON 810.
So far, however, no Estonian farmer has been interested in growing GM crops.
This is due to the fact that there is no maize pest in Estonia, a European stemweed for which the MON 810 maize line is resistant.
Also, varieties of this maize line are bred to the southern regions, and this corn is not suitable for our climatic conditions.
The Ministry of Rural Affairs has commissioned in 2015 and 2016 studies to detect pollution with possible GM organisms in the seeds of various varieties of spring oilseed rape, maize and soy grown in Estonia.
The results of the analysis of the samples taken showed that the seeds did not contain foreign genes, and as a result of the study, it was concluded that the seeds of rape, maize and soy varieties grown in Estonia do not contain genetically modified organisms.
In turn, it can be concluded from the above that genetically modified crops are not deliberately grown in Estonia, and it was also confirmed that there has been no accidental contamination.
Jose Mourinho believes Manchester United have recruited a "genius" in the form of Nemanja Matic
Jose Mourinho believes Manchester United have recruited a "genius" in the form of Nemanja Matic after midfielder Sampdoria made his debut on Wednesday night.
Bought by Chelsea for 40 million pounds, the player began a 2-1 win in the starting line-up on Tuesday ahead of the Uefa Super Cup Real Madrid in the last pre-season game in Dublin, impressinging the defensive role, while Paul Pogba and Andreas Pereira were free to go on offensive rallies.
But apart from Blues' pre-season Asian Tour, Maticul was in desperate need of a matching uniform and only lasted 45 minutes. According to Mourinho, this must definitely be improved before the start of the Premier League season.
"He needs time, but he has experience, intelligence, a brilliant mindset - he has a mood for football," Mourinho said.
"Let's help him get in shape as soon as possible."
Like Matic, Victor Lindelof and Romelu Lukaku, who have been recruited, also started in the starting line-ups at Aviva Stadium, and Mourinho began to quietly set up his own starting man by the beginning of the season.
The Independent revealed this week that Mourinho still wants to recruit three footballers, but he confirmed that the club has already done "excellent work", signing contracts with three of his four priority options.
"I'm grateful to the club that they managed to realize three out of four," he added.
“I’ll be even happier if they can make four out of four, but they’ve worked hard for me.”
Another crane cub received a GSM escort on his leg on 1 August and this time in Muhu.
According to Bird Observer Mati Martinson living in Sõrve, the first stork cub was accompanied by Sõrve, and this litter is already fling around, has left the nesting site and has joined Mullutu-Suurlahe's large collection of overnight stays.
This is because there are no fields of grain in Sõrve, the Voice of the Islands writes.
This Tuesday, it was relatively late to ring cranes, another stork cub came to the attendant, on the island of Muhu.
According to Martinson, the installation of transmitters is actually an international project led by Aivar Leito and Ivar Ojaste.
Last year, a stork son named Mati GSM-escort was equipped with Sõrves Türjul.
"His activities are all available on the Internet: how he went wintering in Spain, when he came back for a long time on the German-Polish border and then came to Saaremaa," Martinson said.
The British inventor had a microchip installed in his body to get into the house and car with a hand gesture
A British inventor became the first person in the country to be fitted with a microchip so that he could open his front door, get into the office and even start a car.
33-year-old Steven Northam had a tiny chip installed between his thumb and forefinger, and now offers this service through his company BioTeq also to companies and individuals who want to make their lives easier with technology.
He has joined forces with Dr. Geoff Watson, a consultant-anaesthesiologist at Hampshire County Royal Hospital in Hants, Winchester, to ensure that the installation procedure complies with medical standards.
The technology is similar to a microchip for cats and dogs, and it only takes 30 seconds.
Northam's company also replaces the locks in houses and cars so that they are compatible with the chip, asking for 300 pounds for one lock.
This married father of one child has now installed technology both at home and in his BMW Z4, which starts when his hand is on the steering wheel.
White House Exhibit Costs Taxpayers Thousands
According to military documents disclosed to TIME magazine, the White House's decision to launch a naval helicopter into the southern yard last year last year for an event celebrating American production cost taxpayers 24,000 dollars.
Green and white-coloured Sikorsky VH-3D, called the Marine One during the president's presence on board, was the central attraction of an event introducing American construction programs at the White House on July 17.
President Donald Trump, Vice-President Mike Pence, and senior officials of the White Housing visited industrial products from all 50 states during their tour.
The helicopter built in Connecticut was exhibited together with a yacht from Maine, a fire truck from Wisconsin, and a forklift made in Mississippi.
According to documents from the Marine Helicopter Squadron One (HMX-1) squadron responsible for operating the helicopter, the White House War Cabinet demanded a partial order of the helicopter's presence with FARGO just 36 hours before the helicopter landed in the southern yard for an "unusual" event.
"I wanted you to be aware of this because it's an unusually high-focused event," the HMX-1 commander said in an e-mailing letter to his boss, deputy leading officer of the Navy in the field of aviation, the day before the event.
It was noted in the message that the helicopter would arrive in the southern yard for an event starting at 7 a.m
What was added was the fact that if leaving the White House were to be delayed, the return flight would require a second crew due to restrictions on working hours.
The original flight crew was by the helicopter at the time of the event, and the squadron's security team remained close to the aircraft throughout its stay on the ground.
It was not possible to find out in the documents given to the magazine TIME whether or not another crew was eventually needed.
Hatch's law prohibits federal workers from using government property for political purposes.
The documents show that HMX-1 examined whether a given event could violate this setting.
"We asked if it violated Hatch's law, and we were told not to violate," the commander wrote.
"You don't seem to have a choice," replied the deputy chief officer.
The White House declared the event "American-made" an official event, and Hatch's law wouldn't have covered it.
But there are political undertones even at official events.
At the event, the president supported the efforts of the health care reform that had been discussed in the Senate at that time and praised his efforts to keep the government's regulations under control.
Using an aircraft known as Marine One or Air Force One for politically profitable purposes is not a new phenomenon.
Presidents must use them to travel.
However, the requirement to use them purely for a photo session is unusual.
Trump came under scrutiny in February when his first campaign event in Melbourne, Florida rolled into the backdrow, and his Twitter account is full of messages complaining about how his predecessor used the president's plane.
"The White House is very pleased with the "American-made", where products from all 50 states manufactured by American workers made of American materials were represented," White House spokeswoman Lindsay Walters said.
HMX-1 teams often make test flights around Washington and in the southern yard to practise laying down at the White House of the United States with centimetre accuracy.
When Trump attended the G20 summit in Germany in early July, HMS-1 organized 58 trial landings in the White House courtyard.
According to the official, the flight to the "America-made" event was included in the norm for the flight crew's training flights of that month.
Squadron helicopters flew for an additional 11 hours that day for "non-presidential support missions."
According to the White House, no other exhibit of this event burdened the taxpayer's wallet.
According to Sikorsky's representative, the company was not involved in the organisation of display in the southern courtyard of the helicopter.
HMX-1 does not keep budget documents for presidential flights, and it's hard to figure out the exact costs caused by the event.
However, according to the Ministry of Defence's hourly rates for the financial year 2017, the VH-3D compensation rate is 24 380 dollars per flight hour.
The squadron's documents marked the length of the flight from its main base at Navy Quantico Airport from Virginia in both directions for 30 minutes.
The White House argues that the actual cost of using a helicopter is well below this amount.
"Calculating the costs of taxpayers based on estimated compensation rates is very misleading," Walters said.
'Those estimated compensation rates shall include staff, maintenance and other irreversible expenditure, which shall be taken into account for the annual appropriations.'
According to the 2015 RAND survey, the compensation rates of the Ministry of Defence do not include personnel costs, but they do cover the costs related to fuel, maintenance and contracts.
Former White House spokeswoman Sean Spicer defended the helicopter's use at the event.
"The point is to show things made in America this week," Spicer said.
"And I know that Sikorsky and Connecticut states are very proud that they are contributing to our national security and that there are presumably hundreds, if not even thousands, of people whose work depends on it.
And I believe that just like most Americans, we're all proud of this helicopter and other military equipment that so many Americans have struggled tirelessly.
Of course, it’s right to highlight it.”
It's not clear whether or not Trump came into contact with a helicopter while taking a tour in the southern yard as part of the event, but he was behind the wheel of the fire truck.
Inside the White House, Trump approached Sikorsky's spokesmans, joking to media outlets about the company's products in his personal fleet.
"I know Sikorsky very well," said the president, "I've got three of their products."
Grit Šadeiko - through tears came peace and joy
At the 2011 World Championships in Daegu, Šadeiko was hit by a leg cramp in the last area, in the 800 m run, and the girl was taken away from the stadium with a stretcher.
Immediately before that, he had reached zero in the long jumping at the Universiate in China.
The 2012 European Championships in Helsinki were ruined by an injury sustained in a cheap throw, but at the London Debut Olympics, he finished 23rd with 6013 points.
Unhappiness, however, continued.
At the 2013 World Championships in Moscow, after the opening day, an Estonian was injured in the long jumping long jump.
Two years later, he finished 15th at the Beijing World Championships and reached the Olympic norm, but he also wanted more to disappoint the athlete.
Šadeiko went through the heaviest gauntlet only last year, when he did not get the result in the long jump at the Amsterdam European Championships.
I'd just like to put together a proper multi-competition once.
Once upon a time," confessed Šadeiko.
Worse was yet to come: a month later, his Olympic competition in Rio was interrupted due to a thigh injury at the first hurdles.
The time had to be taken down - whether to continue with the sequate competition at all?
Is it worth two days of effort?
She thought for a long time, but found peace in herself.
I got my head clear
It's quite difficult to describe this experience, but I've been thinking that last season and the Rio Olympics were something that just had to happen.
Fortunately, there was time to heal yourself and keep your head clear.
"I knew I wouldn't put the pills in my bag yet," Šadeiko says of his self-purification.
I was able to turn a clean sheet and move on.
According to coach Anne Mägi, it was very difficult to look at the suffering of the ward, but he realized that, above all, the athlete had to find out for himself.
He was beaten down by everyone.
It was hard to endure.
"Grit, however, said that he doesn't give up and he wants to prove to himself what he's worth," says Mägi.
Now there's some peace in him.
He's doing this for his own pleasure.
Šadeiko scored a new Estonian record of 6280 points in the Götzis multi-competition.
In high-level competitions, he improved his personal top mark by nearly a metre (13.89) and ran in a hurdles close to the record.
Then the multi-competitor sensed that it was by no means a ceiling of abilities, but only the initial starvation of the competition was erased.
Mägi, who supervised him for many years, says that Šadeiko is a pattern student.
He's ready for an athlete. He's good to train.
If something's in trouble or it's wrong, he'll say it right away.
He knows his body and trusts his cognition.
That's what we need to proceed from.
I can't say exactly what we have to do and let's follow it.
Šadeiko has also found a new approach.
"I'm not the kind of person who makes the maximum in training", he says and says that the most important thing is to get the right cognition, because it also ensures a good result.
The baby's happiness of loved ones makes you happy
Šadeiko has also been given good emotions by the happiness of loved ones.
Both her former training partner Maris Mägi and sister Grete recently became parents, and several competitors have received support from the new citizens of the world.
"It's been pretty interesting," says Šadeiko.
One alcohol, the drinking of which is equivalent to the "training" of the brain.
According to Livestrong, Dr. Gordon Sheperd is such a big fan of wine that he believes that wine can train your brain better than anything else in the world.
Sheperd is a neuroscientist at Yale University.
According to him, it is not about alcohol or how it affects our feelings and thoughts, but in the process of tastinging "very active" wine.
By sniffing a glass, swirling wine in our mouths, enjoying it and perceivingatinginging the aftertaste, we put our brains to work to create a taste for the wine.
According to Sheperd, wine molecules don't actually contain flavor, but the taste arises from the way wine stimulates the brain.
For comparison, he brings how the brain "sees" colors.
"The taste isn't in the wine, the taste creates the taster of the wine," says Sheperd.
The things that still affect the origin of the taste of wine are, for example, our age, gender and even the amount of saliva that a person produces.
Of course, Sheperd admonishes that wine isn't to get drunk.
"There has to be more in action," he says.
If you take too much sip, the system is oversaturated.
Excessive wine-drinking can do harm to the brain instead of benefiting - according to a 2004 study, people who drink more than three glasses of wine, memory loss, loss of balance, and reduced mental capacity.
10% of employees in the Estonian ICT sector get paid less than their counterparts
Data from the recruitment company Fontes reveals that 10% of employees in the Estonian ICT sector receive fewer salaries than their colleagues.
The ten-percent pay gap is between men and women, according to Genius.
59 IT and telecommunications companies participated in Fontes' ICT sector wage survey.
There were 8,577 posts.
63% of the posts were occupied by men.
"Although only 3 companies stated that the wage difference is in favour of men and in the opinion of the rest of the companies there is no pay gap, in reality, compared to similar jobs with each other, the men's monthly basic salary is on average 10% higher than that of women and the total salary of men on average is 8% higher than that of women," said Ilmar Põhjala, an analyst at Fontes.
"Narcos" Season 3 ad clip: Now that Pablo Escobar is gone, the Cali cartel will step down in the Netflix hit series
Despite revealing Pablo Escobar's killer in "Narcos" second season, the Netflix series is far from over.
