According to the United Nations, Afghan democracy depends on the role of women in politics
The head of the U.N. mission in Afghanistan said on Wednesday that elections can only represent the people if all members of society, including women, play a role in them.
Nicholas Haysom, the head of the U.N. relief mission, said that hundreds of women are running in local Soviet elections, three women are running for vice president, and more than 30 percent of recently enrolled voters are women.
"It is not possible to exaggerate the importance of the role of women," he said in a statement on Wednesday.
Elections can truly represent the people and be credible only if women participate fully and are involved in all stages of the electoral process.
Elections and presidential elections in Afghanistan will take place on 5 April.
The Taliban, for their part, have threatened the electoral process, which coincides with a transitional period in which the role of international military operations becomes advisory.
This year's elections mark the first time that power is calmly handed over from one democratically elected government to the next.
According to Haysom, the strong role of women increases the success of democracy in Afghanistan.
According to the Independent Electoral Commission of Afghanistan, 1.2 million out of 3.6 million registered voters are women.
