GMB worries about investments at McVitie's Glasgow plant
The union is awaiting confirmation from the managers of the cookie producer McVities about long-term production plans at the company's Glasgow plant.
According to GMB Scotland, the company has remained silent about the future of the Tollcross investment.
More than 500 people work in the factory, which produces Hobnobs and Rich Tea cookies and other products.
Pladis, the owner of McVitie's company, said they had invested more than 5 million pounds in the site over the past few years.
More employees were hired there, and the chairmans of the trade union "are the first to hear" when something changes in the activities of the factory.
In a letter towned to the company's site manager, GMB Scotland appealed to McVitie to "dissect concerns that the funding would not be used to modernize production lines."
Drew Duffy, GMB's senior organizer, said: "The fact that we don't get clarity from the site's chief manager about Tollcross's business needs and future investment plan makes our members deeply worried.
Several generations of families have worked here, and the workforce is largely made up of the local community and the people around Glasgow, being an indispensable pillar to ensure proper employment in an area with high levels of unemployment and social deprivation.
Thus, in terms of jobs, wages and well-being in the eastern part of Glasgow and against the background of Brexit, the rise in living costs and the chronic decline in production, Glasgow urgently needs a well-thriving-yielding company at McVitie."
Pladis said: "The tollcross site manager received a letter from GMB Scotland only yesterday, and the company responds directly, as we value active communication with employees and trade union representatives.
Two months ago, a regular meeting took place in Tollcross between employees and GMB's representatives, where a number of problems were discussed openly.
We then discussed with our employees that, like all other companies operating in an extremely competitive and precarious economic climate, we are reviewing our activities on an ongoing basis - and this applies to all of our companies and objects - in order to ensure our competitiveness.
He added: "In recent years, we've invested more than 5 million pounds in Tollcross site, most of which have gone into production lines and operational improvements.
We fully understand the heritage of Tollcross as an object of production, and if there should be any changes in the activities of the object, the workers and the representatives of the trade union will be the first to hear about it."
Did PSG warn Barcelona three years ago against approaching Neymar three years ago?
La Liga may regret her interest in Marco Verratti
Paris Saint-Germain will not give up in an effort to lure Neymar into his ranks, making an offer of 189 million pounds to buy him. But did they warn Barcelona three years ago about this record-breaking conquest?
This popular approach has opened a summer transfer window, and the Ligue 1 club is ready to smash more than double the overbuying world record, which was set last season last season with Paul Pogba's return to Manchester United for 89 million pounds.
As a result, Neymar becomes the world's most expensive player and also the highest-paid professional athlete in the world who will receive more than 500 000 pounds a week after payment from PSG.
Barcelona is known to be angry both because of the manner of the approach and for breaking Neymar's freedom, although a source closer to the Catalans board confirms that the club has always taken the best care of a footballer since his arrival from Santos in 2013.
But did PSG's president warn the football world of such an opportunity three years ago?
When Manchester City showed interest in signing Blaise Matuid in 2014, Nasser Al-Khelaif severely warned all clubs who are thinking about recruiting some of PSG's best players.
"I'm not worried, because they know that if they touch one of our players, we'll confront them and contact their players."
"We respect all clubs.
“But if they try to make a contract with one of our players, they will have to face the consequences.”
Barcelona appears to have crossed this border as the Spanish club earlier this summer showed interest in PSG midfielder Marco Verratti.
PSG made it clear that they will not allow the Italian to leave under any circumstances and that Barcelona could suffer a setback as a result of this interest, losing one of their precious assets in the form of Neymar.
Prime Minister: After Brexit, Ireland wants to build bridges, not borders.
"Brex is seriously threatening to wedge between Northern Ireland and Ireland, Great Britain and Ireland," Varadkar warned on his first visit to Northern Ireland after taking office in June.
"I can't imagine who would benefit from it," he added.
Cross-border infrastructure projects made it possible for the current free movement of people and goods, Varadkar said.
"This is our vision for the future: to build bridges, not borders," he stressed.
According to Varadkar, he would prefer Britain to remain part of the European Union, and since this cannot be done, remaining part of the common European market.
It would maintain current free trade between the Republic of Ireland and the United Kingdom.
Another opportunity to streamline Britain's departure would be the creation of a customs union between the European Union and the United Kingdom, the Irish Prime Minister said.
Noting that such an agreement existed with Turkey, Varadkar said: "Certainly, we could have such with Britain."
An alternative to this would be a return to customs offices that would pronounce a historical division and political failure.
"Break to trade, prosperity and peace," the Irish Prime Minister said.
He called Brexit "the challenge of our generation" and warned that its outcome could affect every aspect of Northern Ireland's daily life.
The Irish Prime Minister met with the leaders of Northern Ireland's main political parties while visiting Belfast.
Märt Avandi, who passed his first semi-long triathlon: Everyone can complete the Ironman.
Today, the largest triathlon festival in the Baltics was held in the winter capital of Otepää, when approximately a thousand triathletes from more than thirty countries took part in the semi-long Ironman competition.
The 70.3-mile distance consisted of 1.9 km of swimming, 90 km of cycling and 21.1 km of cycling.
Among others, Märt Avandi finished the first triathlon of his life victoriously.
"I thought it would be difficult and in the end it was difficult," Avandi said at the finish.
Despite the complexity, Avandi admitted that triathlon is actually feasible for almost everyone.
"It's not the distance that kills, the pace kills," he said, hinting that almost everyone will reach the finish line at an affordable pace.
With the exception of Ott Sepp: "Ott's physique is all right, but unfortunately he can't swim."
Avandi had been actively training since February.
At first 4-5 hours a week, but later ten hours a week.
By 05:34:12, he finished 270th overall.
Although Avandi lost to both Raio Piiroja (time 04:59:53) and Jürgen Ligi (time 05:06:37), he defeated other so-called "prominent class" contestants.
Tanel Padar, who was the "person of a good example" last year, lost to Avandi in seven minutes (time 05:41:54), journalist Sten Teppan in 16 minutes (time 05:50:23), Kalev Kruus and Raivo E. Tamm in about an hour (times 06:31:53 and 06:45:18, respectively).
Johannes Moldan of Germany was crowned the overall winner of the men's competition of the Otepää stage of the Ironman series with a time of 3:58:27, the best of the Estonians was Ivo Suur with a time of 04:14:09.
Among the women, Helle Frederiksen of Denmark won with a time of 04:24:01, while Maria Jänese, who lost to Frederiksen in almost half an hour and finished at 04:58:58.
Avandi completed the 1.9 km swimming distance in 42 minutes and 14 seconds.
He came out of the water at 410.
